#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""testslot: one machine-wide test gate on a Linux host (and on a Mac), shared by every account and repo.

  testslot run [--cpus N | --heavy | --track] [--max-minutes N] [--key KEY] [--log FILE] -- <command...>
  testslot suggest [--default N] (--key KEY | -- <command...>)
  testslot status [--json]
  testslot summary [--since 12h] [--json]
  testslot stop [--kill] <pgid>

Admission is by CPU. Each run declares the cpus it uses (--cpus N, its -j; default 1). Runs start
first come, first served while the charged cpus of everything running, plus the new run's, stay
within the cap (the number in $DIR/cap, or TESTSLOT_CAP; with neither, max(1, round(cores * 0.8)); as of 2026-10-20 a Linux host 20, a Mac 6), and while the
1-minute load is under the load cap ($DIR/load-cap, or TESTSLOT_LOAD_CAP, else the cap; as of 2026-10-20 a Linux host 10, a Mac 10), so outside work (builds, sessions) counts too; and while another
run is going, that load plus the new run's cpus also stays within the load cap.
--heavy means cpus = the cap: the box to itself. --track takes no cpus at all: it is only listed, for
long jobs that are API calls rather than CPU (its load, like any outside work's, still counts in the
load gate). A run asking for more than the cap gets the cap.

Runs start first come, first served among runs of a size, but a run may be backfilled: a run that fits in the free
cpus (and the load) starts ahead of a bigger run waiting before it, so a 10-cpu run at the head doesn't idle the box
while it waits for a 4-cpu run to end. A run is never passed by one asking for as many cpus or more. Once the run at
the head has waited past the reserve time ($DIR/reserve or TESTSLOT_RESERVE, seconds; default 600), the queue reserves
cpus for it: nothing passes it, and it starts as soon as enough cpus free. A head that the load holds back while anything
holds (its cpus on top of the load go over the load cap) can start only on an idle slot, so it reserves after six times
that wait (a --heavy run keeps the plain one): smaller runs backfill past it until then, and after it they wait for the
head too, so it can't starve. status and summary name the run cpus are reserved for, and mark a run that started by
backfill.
A run that would start while another waits ahead of it (a head: waiting for cpus or for the load, reserved or not) needs
a time limit, --max-minutes N (decimal minutes, above 0). A run with no limit, or one over the backfill limit
($DIR/backfill-limit or TESTSLOT_BACKFILL_LIMIT, minutes; default 30), waits for the head (wait reason no-limit). A
limited run starts if it fits; against a reserved head its limit must end within the backfill limit of the moment the
head began to reserve. A run that started that way is stopped, as a cancel with stop_reason "time limit" in its
history line, once its limit passes while a run it started past still waits. That stop is final: unlike the load requeue
below, the run is not started again. A run that starts with nothing waiting
ahead is never stopped for its limit.
Outside load that stays at or over the cap holds the whole queue until it falls; status says so. The
turnstile ($DIR/turnstile) is held only for the moment of each admission check. Runs also hold the old
flock gate, $DIR/heavy (LOCK_EX for --heavy, LOCK_SH otherwise), so Cat Herder's tools/test-parallel,
which takes LOCK_EX on $CAT_HERDER_TEST_SLOT (both accounts point it at $DIR/heavy), still queues
against them. A command run under testslot gets TESTSLOT_HELD (kind, wrapper pid and start), which Cat Herder's
test-parallel reads since 0.53.3, so a nested test-parallel doesn't wait on its own parent; a nested testslot
sees its ancestor's lock in /proc/locks and runs inside the parent's admission. When the command's main process ends, the rest of its process group is
ended too; on Linux that includes anything in the command's cgroup, a child that called setsid among it. A run whose
wrapper was killed outright (an orphan) holds its cpus against the cap while its process group lives, and on Linux,
where the command ran in a cgroup, while anything lives in that cgroup: a setsid child that left the group used to free
the slot while it ran on. Stop a run with `testslot stop <pgid>`, which goes through its wrapper and counts as a
cancel; a signal sent to the group alone (kill -TERM -- -<pgid>) looks like the command dying, a failure.

Start-rate throttle: with a cap on process starts ($DIR/start-rate-cap, else TESTSLOT_START_RATE_CAP, else none: no
throttle; the Mac's setup writes 120), each run's wrapper reads the host's starts a second, every second (Linux: the
processes counter in /proc/stat; macOS: the pid of a child it starts, since pids are handed out in order). Over the cap,
averaged over 3 s, it stops its own group (and cgroup) for up to 3 s. If the rate falls by half the cap meanwhile, the
burst was its own: it resumes once under 80% of the cap and may pause again; if not, the starts are outside work, and
the run resumes and isn't paused again for 60 s. A paused run keeps its full cpus, prints a line on its first pause and
at most every 30 s after, and its history line counts pauses, paused_s and max_pause_s. Every end path (stop, a cancel,
a killed wrapper's orphan) resumes the group before it signals it. TESTSLOT_FAKE_START_RATE names a file holding the
host rate, for tests.

Wait reason: a waiting run records why it waits as one code, in its holder, in status (--json and the "(waiting: <code>)" on its
row) and, once admitted, on its history line as wait_reason: reserved (cpus are reserved for the head), queued-ahead (runs
queued ahead of it), cpus (the cap is in use), load (the load cap), gate (the test gate is held outside testslot or by a
heavy run) or no-limit (a run is waiting ahead and this one has no --max-minutes, or one over the backfill limit). It is
empty on a run that never waited, and status shows it only while the run waits. A run with a limit shows it on its status
row as "(limit 2m, 95s left)", and in --json as limit_s and limit_left_s.

Load pause: while the 1-minute load is over 1.5 times the cores, the one light run using the most cpus above what it
declared pauses itself (SIGSTOP to its group and cgroup), and resumes once the load is under the load cap. A heavy or
tracked run, a run with no reading, and the last run going are never paused. If the load stays over the trigger, the
next worst over-user pauses at the next second. It prints a line when it pauses and when it resumes, and status shows
it as "paused: load". It is kept apart from the start-rate pause: a run under both is resumed only when both have
cleared. TESTSLOT_OBSERVED_DIR names a folder with one file per run, named by its process group id, holding that run's
cpu reading (TESTSLOT_OBSERVED is the fallback), for tests.

Requeue, the last resort after the load pause: a light run whose last unpaused reading is over twice what it declared,
and that the load pause first paused 5 minutes ago (TESTSLOT_REQUEUE_AFTER_S, for tests; the 5 minutes count from that
first pause since it went over, never from when it went over, so the pause always has its turn first; time spent paused counts toward them), while the load is
still over 1.5 times the cores and another run is going, is stopped by its own wrapper as a cancel (the history line says cancel) and started again in the same process, so the caller keeps waiting
on the same command and sees only the final run's exit code. The new run goes to the head of the queue, declaring its
measured peak rounded up (held to the cap), prints why on stderr and in the log, and shows "requeued once" in status. A
run is requeued once; a second overrun is left to the load pause. A heavy or tracked run, one with no reading, and the
last run going are never requeued. The output of the first run stays in the log and on the caller's stdout. The
internal option --requeued DECLARED:PEAK is how the wrapper restarts itself; it is not for callers.

A run's peak observed cpu (see the sampler) is recorded in its history line under a key: --key KEY, else the
command with its paths, hex and number tokens and --log argument dropped. `testslot suggest` prints the last
peak for a key as a whole number of cpus, for the next run's --cpus, or nothing (or --default N) when no run with
that key has a reading; it always exits 0 unless its own arguments are wrong. A key past 200 characters is stored
as a hash, so a caller can pass any length.

Idle shrink (as of 2026-10-21: N = 600 s, margin = 1 cpu; $DIR/shrink-after and $DIR/shrink-margin, else TESTSLOT_SHRINK_AFTER
and TESTSLOT_SHRINK_MARGIN, validated and clamped, garbage falling back to the default): a run counted by cgroup whose
observed cpu has stayed under its declared cpus for N seconds is charged observed + margin, never above its declaration
and never under 1, so an idle 8-cpu run frees most of its cpus for waiting runs. The charge follows the reading while it
stays under the declaration (0.1 -> 1.1, 2.0 -> 3) and is the full declaration again once the reading climbs back to
the declaration, at the next check. Only light runs counted by cgroup shrink: a process-group reading undercounts, and
heavy and --track runs never shrink. THE WORST CASE, plainly: a shrunk run is charged seen + margin while it may hold
its whole declaration, and the cpus it frees can be filled. If it then climbs to its declaration, real use is above
the cap until the admitted runs end: nothing enforces the cap on running runs, only the load gate stops new starts.
Shrunk runs add up: each can free declared - margin - seen cpus, so a 4-cpu run at 0.1 frees 2.9, and five of them on
the 20-cpu host free 14.5; if those five climb to 4 each while the 14.5 is filled, demand is 34.5 cpus on a 20-cpu
box, 1.7 times the cap, until the admitted runs end. The reading lags by one averaged sample interval plus the write, about 3 to 6 s at the
defaults, so a burst takes up to two samples to show. The wrapper rewrites a shrunk record every sample; one not
rewritten for 4 sample intervals + 1 s (a dead sampler) is charged in full.

Each run writes a holder file ($DIR/holders/<pid>.json) that status reads. A dead wrapper's record still counts
while its command's group or cgroup lives; otherwise it is ignored there and deleted by the next run. When a run ends, one line goes into
$DIR/history.jsonl (queued, started, ended, exit, cpus and more), which summary reads. The command
runs in its own process group, and its output goes to the terminal and to a log ($DIR/logs/ unless
--log).

On macOS (a Mac takes small runs while the Linux host is full) the same commands work against
/Users/Shared/testslot, set up by testslot/setup-mac.sh (the live cap and load-cap are the numbers in $DIR/cap and $DIR/load-cap; as of 2026-10-20 a Mac 6/10): $DIR/load-cap (or
TESTSLOT_LOAD_CAP) is the load the gate holds at, the cap when unset. ps stands in for /proc; a nested
run knows its parent by TESTSLOT_HELD (kind, wrapper pid and start), since there is no /proc/locks; and
on macOS status lists a test-parallel run outside testslot by its command line (as Linux does), but no other
holder outside testslot.

Exit codes: run returns its command's code (128+n if it was killed by signal n, or cancelled with n);
2 for a usage error, 1 for a setup or permission error, 127 when the command can't start, and 70
(INTERNAL_ERROR, sysexits' EX_SOFTWARE) when testslot itself fails on something it didn't expect: it prints
"testslot error: ..." on stderr and exits 70, so a wrapper that reads only the code can tell a broken gate from
a failed suite. A command may itself exit 70, so the stderr line is the only reliable marker of testslot's own
failure. Once the command has finished, an internal error after that (in the cleanup) keeps the command's exit code
and still prints the line. A closed stdout (testslot status | head) ends quietly with 141, not as an error. Everything
printed is written with errors="replace", so a path or command line that isn't UTF-8 shows as "?" and cannot crash a
listing. Test hooks: TESTSLOT_TEST_CRASH=<text> raises an internal error at start, except after-wait raises it just after
the command ends, and pipe raises BrokenPipeError (quiet exit 141). TESTSLOT_DEBUG=1 adds the traceback after the
"testslot error" line. status, summary and stop return 0 (stop: 1 if the group survives).
"""
import datetime
import fcntl
import getpass
import hashlib
import json
import math
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time

# Linux reads /proc; macOS (a Mac, for small runs while the Linux host is full) asks ps instead.
# TESTSLOT_PLATFORM=darwin makes Linux take the ps path too, so the tests on Linux reach it.
PLATFORM = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_PLATFORM") or ("linux" if sys.platform.startswith("linux") else "darwin")
LINUX = PLATFORM == "linux"
if LINUX and not sys.platform.startswith("linux"):
    PLATFORM, LINUX = "darwin", False  # no /proc here: TESTSLOT_PLATFORM=linux can only mean the ps path
DIR = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_DIR", "/var/lib/testslot" if sys.platform.startswith("linux") else "/Users/Shared/testslot")
HEAVY = os.path.join(DIR, "heavy")
TURNSTILE = os.path.join(DIR, "turnstile")
HOLDERS = os.path.join(DIR, "holders")
LOGS = os.path.join(DIR, "logs")
HISTORY = os.path.join(DIR, "history.jsonl")
LOADAVG = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_LOADAVG", "/proc/loadavg" if LINUX else "")  # tests point it at a file of their own
CORES = os.cpu_count() or 2
# Observed cpu: each wrapper samples its run's cgroup, or its process group as a fallback, every SAMPLE_S seconds
# (3 unless set; tests set it small). TESTSLOT_OBSERVED names a file holding one number: tests use it as every run's reading instead.
try:
    SAMPLE_S = float(os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_SAMPLE_S") or 3)
    SAMPLE_S = min(3600.0, max(0.5, SAMPLE_S)) if math.isfinite(SAMPLE_S) else 3.0  # 0 would spin it, inf would kill it
except ValueError:
    SAMPLE_S = 3.0
OBSERVED_FILE = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_OBSERVED", "")
# tests: a folder with one file per run, named by the run's process group id; a run reads its own file first and falls
# back to TESTSLOT_OBSERVED when it has none, so one run can be set apart from honest ones.
OBSERVED_DIR = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_OBSERVED_DIR", "")
VIA_HOOK = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_OBSERVED_VIA", "")  # tests: with TESTSLOT_OBSERVED, record these readings as a cgroup's
# The longest a run waits for systemd-run to start its command in a scope before it stops the launcher and runs the
# command without one (10 s unless set; tests set it small). Starting a scope takes some milliseconds.
try:
    LAUNCH_S = float(os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_LAUNCH_S") or 10)
    LAUNCH_S = min(300.0, max(1.0, LAUNCH_S)) if math.isfinite(LAUNCH_S) else 10.0
except ValueError:
    LAUNCH_S = 10.0
# Linux runs each command in its own systemd user scope (a cgroup), so the sampler counts every process the run
# ever started, short-lived and setsid ones included. TESTSLOT_NO_CGROUP=1 forces the process-group path (tests).
CGROUP_ROOT = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_CGROUP_ROOT") or "/sys/fs/cgroup"  # a test hook (documented, like TESTSLOT_SAMPLE_S): the suite points it at a folder of its own
NO_CGROUP = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_NO_CGROUP") == "1"


# debt: the last good values are kept per process, so a process that starts during the truncate window of
# `echo N > cap` has none. One read retries a few times (4 more, 30 ms apart) before taking the default, but only
# when the file is there and bad, and a result is reused for a quarter second (monotonic clock), so a check pays
# for one read of each file, not one per holder. Upgrade: write the cap files with a rename, so no reader ever sees them empty.
_LAST_CAP = None  # the last cap read cleanly by this process; a cap file empty for an instant keeps it
_LAST_LOAD_CAP = None
_CAP_CACHE = {}  # name -> (when, value)
CAP_TTL = 0.25


def _cached(name, read):
    hit = _CAP_CACHE.get(name)
    if hit and time.monotonic() - hit[0] < CAP_TTL:
        return hit[1]
    value = read()
    _CAP_CACHE[name] = (time.monotonic(), value)
    return value


def _read_cap():
    global _LAST_CAP
    path = os.path.join(DIR, "cap")
    for attempt in range(1 if _LAST_CAP else 5):
        if attempt:
            time.sleep(0.03)
        for src in (_read(path), os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_CAP")):
            try:
                if src and int(src.strip()) >= 1:  # int() refuses inf and nan
                    _LAST_CAP = int(src.strip())
                    return _LAST_CAP
            except ValueError:
                pass
        if not os.path.exists(path) and not os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_CAP"):
            break  # no file and no override: nothing is being written, so don't wait for it
    return _LAST_CAP or max(1, round(CORES * 0.8))


def read_cap():
    """The cpu cap: the number in $DIR/cap (shared, so every account admits against the same one), else
    TESTSLOT_CAP (for tests, in a scratch folder with no cap file), else the last good value read by this
    process, else max(1, round(CORES * 0.8)). Read afresh each check, so a change reaches runs already waiting."""
    return _cached("cap", _read_cap)


def _read(path):
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            return f.read()
    except OSError:
        return None



def _read_load_cap():
    global _LAST_LOAD_CAP
    path = os.path.join(DIR, "load-cap")
    for attempt in range(1 if _LAST_LOAD_CAP else 3):
        if attempt:
            time.sleep(0.03)
        for src in (_read(path), os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_LOAD_CAP")):
            try:
                if src and math.isfinite(float(src.strip())) and float(src.strip()) > 0:
                    _LAST_LOAD_CAP = float(src.strip())
                    return _LAST_LOAD_CAP
            except ValueError:
                pass
        if not os.path.exists(path) and not os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_LOAD_CAP"):
            break  # no file and no override: the cpu cap is the answer, no need to wait for a write
    return _LAST_LOAD_CAP or float(read_cap())


def read_load_cap():
    """The load the gate holds at: $DIR/load-cap, else TESTSLOT_LOAD_CAP, else the last good value read by this
    process, else the cpu cap. The files hold the live numbers: /Users/Shared/testslot/cap and load-cap on the
    Mac, /var/lib/testslot on Linux (provisioning writes 20 and 10 if missing). As of 2026-10-20: a Mac 6/10,
    a Linux host 20/10. Herd runs declare more cpus than they use, so the load cap does the guarding."""
    return _cached("load-cap", _read_load_cap)


def read_reserve():
    """Seconds the run at the head of the queue may be passed by smaller runs: $DIR/reserve, else
    TESTSLOT_RESERVE, else 600. Past it, the queue reserves cpus for that run."""
    for src in (_read(os.path.join(DIR, "reserve")), os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_RESERVE")):
        try:
            if src and math.isfinite(float(src.strip())) and float(src.strip()) >= 0:
                return float(src.strip())
        except ValueError:
            pass
    return 600.0


RESERVE = read_reserve()


def read_backfill_limit():
    """Minutes a run that starts past a waiting head may be given: $DIR/backfill-limit, else TESTSLOT_BACKFILL_LIMIT, else 30."""
    for src in (_read(os.path.join(DIR, "backfill-limit")), os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_BACKFILL_LIMIT")):
        try:
            if src and math.isfinite(float(src.strip())) and float(src.strip()) > 0:
                return float(src.strip())
        except ValueError:
            pass
    return 30.0


def _read_number(name, env, default, lo, hi):
    """A number from $DIR/<name>, else $<env>, else default, clamped to [lo, hi]; anything that isn't a finite number
    (text, nan, inf, an empty file) falls through to the next source, and in the end to the default."""
    for src in (_read(os.path.join(DIR, name)), os.environ.get(env)):
        try:
            if src and math.isfinite(float(src.strip())):
                return min(hi, max(lo, float(src.strip())))
        except ValueError:
            pass
    return default


# Idle shrink: a run counted by cgroup whose observed cpu has stayed under what it declared for SHRINK_AFTER_S seconds
# is charged its observed cpu plus SHRINK_MARGIN, never above its declaration and never below 1. Read once at start,
# like RESERVE. As of 2026-10-21 the defaults are 600 s and 1 cpu.
SHRINK_AFTER_S = _read_number("shrink-after", "TESTSLOT_SHRINK_AFTER", 600.0, 1.0, 86400.0)
SHRINK_MARGIN = _read_number("shrink-margin", "TESTSLOT_SHRINK_MARGIN", 1.0, 0.0, 64.0)
MY_QUEUED = None  # when this run queued, so waiters name only runs queued ahead of it

# Start-rate throttle: the Mac hung when test runs started 200 to 340 processes a second,
# with the cpu and load caps holding the whole time. Each run's wrapper reads the host's process starts every
# START_RATE_S; when the 3-second average is over the cap it stops its own group for up to PAUSE_MAX_S, and keeps it
# stopped only while the rate falls (the burst was its own). A rate that doesn't fall is outside work: the run
# resumes and isn't paused again for HOLD_OFF_S.
START_RATE_S = 1.0
START_RATE_WINDOW = 3     # readings averaged for the cap check
PAUSE_MAX_S = 3.0
HOLD_OFF_S = 60.0
PAUSE_REPORT_S = 30.0     # at most one stderr line per this while a run keeps being paused
FAKE_START_RATE = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_FAKE_START_RATE", "")  # tests: a file holding the host rate, as TESTSLOT_OBSERVED is for cpu
# XNU hands out pids in order: forkproc() in bsd/kern/kern_fork.c does `if (++pid >= PID_MAX) pid = 100;` with
# PID_MAX 99999 (bsd/sys/proc_internal.h), so the highest pid it gives is 99998 and one cycle is 100..99998, 99899 pids.
# debt: forkproc() also skips pids still in use (a live process, group or session), so an advance can overcount by those,
# mostly long-lived daemons; a skip over many is rare. Upgrade: sysctl(KERN_PROC) for the kernel's own count.
PID_MAX = 99999
PID_WRAP_TO = 100
PID_CYCLE = PID_MAX - PID_WRAP_TO   # 99899: pids 100 to 99998
_LAST_RATE_CAP = None


def _read_start_rate_cap():
    global _LAST_RATE_CAP
    path = os.path.join(DIR, "start-rate-cap")
    for src in (_read(path), os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_START_RATE_CAP")):
        try:
            if src and src.strip() and math.isfinite(float(src.strip())) and float(src.strip()) > 0:
                _LAST_RATE_CAP = float(src.strip())
                return _LAST_RATE_CAP
        except ValueError:
            pass
    if not os.path.exists(path) and not os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_START_RATE_CAP"):
        return None  # no file and no override: no throttle
    return _LAST_RATE_CAP  # a file half-written this instant keeps the last good value


def read_start_rate_cap():
    """Process starts a second, for the whole host, over which a run pauses itself: $DIR/start-rate-cap, else
    TESTSLOT_START_RATE_CAP, else None (no throttle). setup-mac.sh writes 120 on a Mac; Linux has none."""
    return _cached("start-rate-cap", _read_start_rate_cap)


def pid_starts(old, new):
    """Processes started between two macOS probe children with pids old and new: the pids handed out between them,
    across a wrap, less the second probe itself."""
    return (new - old) % PID_CYCLE - 1


class StartRate:
    """The host's process starts a second since the last reading. Linux: the `processes` counter in /proc/stat
    (every fork and clone, threads included). macOS: the pid of a child started for the purpose, since pids are
    handed out in order, so short-lived starts count too. None until two readings, or when a reading fails."""
    # debt: on macOS each running wrapper's probe adds about one start a second to every other wrapper's reading;
    # the bias is accepted (a few starts against a cap of 120). Upgrade: subtract the count of throttling wrappers.

    def __init__(self):
        self.last = None  # (counter, monotonic time)

    def counter(self):
        if not LINUX:
            p = subprocess.Popen(["/usr/bin/true"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                 stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            p.wait()
            return p.pid
        with open("/proc/stat", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith("processes "):
                    return int(line.split()[1])
        return None

    def read(self):
        if FAKE_START_RATE:
            try:
                return float((_read(FAKE_START_RATE) or "").strip())
            except ValueError:
                return None
        try:
            c, now = self.counter(), time.monotonic()
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            c, now = None, time.monotonic()
        last, self.last = self.last, (None if c is None else (c, now))
        if c is None or last is None or now <= last[1]:
            return None
        starts = pid_starts(last[0], c) if not LINUX else max(0, c - last[0])
        return max(0.0, starts / (now - last[1]))
# debt: logs are pruned by age only (14 days, own files, at each run); a burst of huge logs could
# still fill the disk. Upgrade: a size cap in prune_logs.
LOG_DAYS = 14
# history.jsonl rolls over to history.jsonl.1 past this size (one old generation is kept): as of 2026-10-20 a line
# is about 500 bytes on average (a Linux host 497, a Mac 546), up to some 750 with a long key and a long command, so
# 7,000 to 10,000 runs each, and summary sees a few weeks back.
HISTORY_MAX = int(os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_HISTORY_MAX", 5 * 1024 * 1024))  # tests set it small


def loadavg():
    """The 1, 5 and 15-minute load averages."""
    if not LOADAVG:
        return list(os.getloadavg())
    try:
        return [float(x) for x in (_read(LOADAVG) or "").split()[:3]] or [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]
    except ValueError:
        return [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]


def cpus_of(h):
    """The cpus a holder record counts for: the size it asked for, held to the cap as it is now, so a cap that
    moves while it waits or runs moves its size too; a record from before --cpus counts by its kind.
    A run still going when the cap is lowered counts at the lowered size, so the box can hold more than the
    cap until it ends: running runs are left alone."""
    if h.get("kind") == "track":
        return 0
    if h.get("kind") == "heavy":
        return read_cap()  # the box to itself, whatever the cap is now
    if isinstance(h.get("cpus"), int):
        return min(h["cpus"], read_cap())  # the size asked for, held to the cap as it stands now
    return 1


def valid_observed(h):
    """The reading a holder's wrapper last recorded, in cores, or None when there is no usable one: none yet
    (an old record, or a run not yet sampled), not a finite number in range (hand-edited, or a failed read),
    or an orphan's (its wrapper is gone, so the reading is stale)."""
    seen = h.get("observed")
    if isinstance(seen, bool) or not isinstance(seen, (int, float)) or h.get("orphan"):
        return None
    if not math.isfinite(seen) or seen < 0 or seen > 4 * max(CORES, read_cap()):
        return None
    return float(seen)


def charged_cpus(h):
    """What a holder costs the cap: the larger of what it declared and what its wrapper last saw it use, in
    cores, held to the cap as it is now (a heavy run is the whole cap). With no usable reading, what it declared."""
    declared = cpus_of(h)
    if pausing(h):
        return declared  # a paused run takes its cpus back on resume: nothing may be admitted into them
    seen = valid_observed(h)
    if seen is None:
        return declared
    if shrunk(h, declared, seen):
        margin = h.get("shrink_margin")  # the margin as the run's wrapper read it, so every reader charges the same
        if isinstance(margin, bool) or not isinstance(margin, (int, float)) or not 0 <= margin <= 64:
            margin = SHRINK_MARGIN
        return min(declared, max(1.0, seen + margin))
    return min(max(declared, seen), read_cap())


def pausing(h):
    """Whether a holder's run is paused by the start-rate throttle or by the load pause, or resumed within
    max(SAMPLE_S, 0.5) + START_RATE_S seconds (its cpu sample may still include paused time, so its low reading
    says nothing about what the run uses)."""
    for k in ("paused_since", "load_paused_since"):
        if isinstance(h.get(k), (int, float)) and not isinstance(h.get(k), bool):
            return True
    back = h.get("resumed_at")
    return (isinstance(back, (int, float)) and not isinstance(back, bool)
            and 0 <= time.time() - back <= max(SAMPLE_S, 0.5) + START_RATE_S)


def record_shrink_after(h):
    """N as a holder's wrapper recorded it, for status: a finite number of seconds in the clamp range, else this process's."""
    n = h.get("shrink_after")
    if isinstance(n, bool) or not isinstance(n, (int, float)) or not math.isfinite(n) or not 1 <= n <= 86400:
        return SHRINK_AFTER_S
    return float(n)


def shrunk(h, declared, seen):
    """Whether a holder is charged its observed cpu plus the margin, not its declaration: its wrapper has seen it under
    its declaration for SHRINK_AFTER_S (`shrunk` in the record, rewritten every sample), the reading is a cgroup's (a
    process-group reading undercounts, so it never shrinks), and the latest reading is still under the declaration, so
    one that climbs back to its declaration is charged in full at once, without waiting for the wrapper's next write.
    A record whose wrapper has not rewritten it for 4 sample intervals + 1 s (its sampler died) is charged in full."""
    if not (h.get("shrunk") is True and h.get("observed_via") == "cgroup" and h.get("kind") == "light" and seen < declared):
        return False
    stamp, every = h.get("sampled_at"), h.get("sample_s")
    if isinstance(stamp, bool) or not isinstance(stamp, (int, float)) or not math.isfinite(stamp):
        return False
    if isinstance(every, bool) or not isinstance(every, (int, float)) or not math.isfinite(every):
        every = SAMPLE_S
    # a record's own interval counts for at most 4 times this process's, so a forged one can't keep a charge shrunk for hours
    # debt: wall clock, since the stamp crosses processes; a clock that jumps only ever makes the charge full again
    return abs(time.time() - stamp) <= 4 * min(max(every, 0.5), 4 * SAMPLE_S) + 1


def holds_cpus(h):
    """Admitted and not finished: its cpus count against the cap (an orphaned command still burns them)."""
    return h.get("state") in ("admitted", "running") and h.get("kind") != "track"


RAN = [None]  # the exit code of a command that has finished, so an internal error after it can't replace it
INTERNAL_ERROR = 70  # no test runner uses it for a failed suite; see the exit codes in the docstring


def die(msg, code=2):
    print(f"testslot: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(code)


def open_lock(path):
    """Open a lock file, creating it group-writable (0660) if missing, so the other account can open it too."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o660)
        os.fchmod(fd, 0o660)  # umask 022 would otherwise drop the group's write bit
        return fd
    except FileExistsError:
        pass
    try:
        return os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_NOFOLLOW)
    except PermissionError:
        return os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)  # flock works on a read-only descriptor


_PS = (-math.inf, {})  # not 0.0: a clock near 0 (a fresh boot, a sandbox) would take this empty table for a fresh listing
_PS_FAILED = -1e9  # when ps last failed three times: for 2s after, callers hear so at once, not after 30s more


class PsFailed(Exception):
    """ps gave no usable listing, three times. Callers fail closed: a process counts as alive (its slot
    stays taken, its group is still signalled), and a run that can't tell, before it holds a slot, stops."""


def ps_table():
    """pid -> (state, ppid, pgid, start) for every process, from one ps call, reused for 0.2s: the
    macOS stand-in for /proc. start is ps's lstart text, which is all a reused pid check needs."""
    # debt: a ps that hangs (not fails) still costs about 16s per check once the 2s failure cache runs
    # out, so teardown under a hung ps can take a minute or two. Upgrade: sysctl(KERN_PROC) through ctypes.
    global _PS, _PS_FAILED
    now = time.monotonic()
    if now - _PS[0] < 0.2:
        return _PS[1]
    if now - _PS_FAILED < 2:
        raise PsFailed()
    # A failed ps (fork EAGAIN, a timeout) must never read as "no processes": every holder would look
    # dead, so runs would be admitted over the cap and live holders pruned. An older table won't do either
    # (anything newer is missing from it). So: retry, then raise PsFailed, and each caller fails closed.
    for attempt in range(3):
        table = {}
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-Ao", "pid=,ppid=,pgid=,stat=,lstart="], capture_output=True,
                               text=True, errors="replace", timeout=5, env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL="C"))
            out = r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else ""
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            out = ""
        for line in out.splitlines():
            f = line.split(None, 4)
            if len(f) == 5 and f[0].isdigit():
                table[int(f[0])] = (f[3][0], int(f[1]), int(f[2]), " ".join(f[4].split()))
        if os.getpid() in table:  # a real listing always has this process in it
            _PS = (now, table)
            return table
        time.sleep(0.2 * (attempt + 1))
    _PS_FAILED = time.monotonic()
    raise PsFailed()


def proc_stat(pid):
    """(state, start time) of a pid, or None if it is gone: from /proc on Linux (start in clock ticks),
    from ps elsewhere (start as ps prints it)."""
    if not LINUX:
        p = ps_table().get(pid)
        return (p[0], p[3]) if p else None
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            fields = f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()
        return fields[0], int(fields[19])
    except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError):
        return None


def signal_reaches(probe):
    """Without ps, the kernel still says whether a pid or group exists: signal 0 checks, sends nothing.
    A zombie counts as present here, so this errs toward alive."""
    try:
        probe()
        return True
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except OSError:
        return True  # exists, but another account's


def alive(pid, start=None):
    """A live process: present in /proc and not a zombie (a killed wrapper nobody has reaped yet).
    Given the start time a holder recorded, a reused pid (another process since) counts as gone."""
    try:
        st = proc_stat(pid)
    except PsFailed:
        return signal_reaches(lambda: os.kill(pid, 0))  # can't check its start: a live pid counts
    return bool(st) and st[0] != "Z" and (start is None or st[1] == start)


def stopped(pid):
    """Stopped (Ctrl-Z, SIGSTOP): a waiting run in this state gives up its place rather than block the queue."""
    try:
        st = proc_stat(pid)
    except PsFailed:
        return False  # not known to be stopped: it keeps its place
    return bool(st) and st[0] in ("T", "t")


def group_alive(pgid):
    """Whether any process is still in process group pgid (its leader may have exited)."""
    if not LINUX:
        try:
            return any(p[2] == pgid and p[0] != "Z" for p in ps_table().values())
        except PsFailed:
            return signal_reaches(lambda: os.killpg(pgid, 0))
    for d in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if d.isdigit():
            try:
                with open(f"/proc/{d}/stat", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                    st = f.read()
            except OSError:
                continue
            try:
                f = st.rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()
                if int(f[2]) == pgid and f[0] != "Z":
                    return True
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                continue
    return False


def ancestors():
    """Pids of this process's ancestors, nearest first."""
    out, pid = [], os.getpid()
    if not LINUX:
        table = ps_table()
        while pid > 1 and pid in table and len(out) < 200:
            pid = table[pid][1]
            out.append(pid)
        return out
    while pid > 1:
        try:
            with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                pid = int(f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[1])
        except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError):
            break
        out.append(pid)
    return out


def holds_running(pid, kind, start):
    """Whether pid's holder record says it is an admitted or running testslot wrapper of this kind, started then."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(HOLDERS, f"{pid}.json"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            h = json.load(f)
        # "admitted" too: the command (and so a nested run) can start before the wrapper writes "running"
        return h.get("state") in ("admitted", "running") and h.get("kind") == kind and str(h.get("start")) == start
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return False


def held_by_ancestor():
    """'heavy' or 'light' if one of our ancestors holds the gate (WRITE or READ in /proc/locks), else ''.
    The lock table is the proof, not an inherited variable: a daemon started under a run keeps the
    variables for life, long after the run's slot is gone.
    Without /proc/locks (macOS) the proof is TESTSLOT_HELD="<kind>:<wrapper pid>:<its start>", checked
    against our live ancestors and the wrapper's own holder record: a daemon that outlived its run, or
    was reparented, no longer counts, and neither does an ancestor that isn't a running wrapper (pid 1).
    Like the gate itself, this is cooperative: an account that forges a holder record skips the queue."""
    if not LINUX:
        kind, _, rest = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_HELD", "").partition(":")
        wpid, _, start = rest.partition(":")
        if (kind in ("heavy", "light") and wpid.isdigit() and int(wpid) > 1 and int(wpid) in ancestors()
                and alive(int(wpid), start) and holds_running(int(wpid), kind, start)):
            return kind
        # debt: no lock table here, so a run nested under test-parallel's own LOCK_EX (not under testslot)
        # would wait on its parent. On the Mac nothing points CAT_HERDER_TEST_SLOT at this gate's heavy
        # file, so test-parallel never takes it. Since 0.53.3 test-parallel sets TESTSLOT_HELD="local:<pid>:<t>",
        # which the kind check above rejects. Upgrade: accept kind "local" once a run can prove it.
        return ""
    locks = heavy_lockers()
    real = os.path.realpath(HEAVY)
    for pid in ancestors():
        if pid in locks and has_open(pid, real):
            return "heavy" if locks[pid] == "WRITE" else "light"
    return ""


def has_open(pid, path):
    """Whether pid has path open: rules out a same-inode flock on another filesystem. An ancestor of
    another account can't be read; trust the lock table then."""
    try:
        fds = os.listdir(f"/proc/{pid}/fd")
    except OSError:
        return True
    for fd in fds:
        try:
            if os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/{fd}") == path:
                return True
        except OSError:
            pass
    return False


def read_holders():
    """Live wrapper records and orphans with a live process group or cgroup, oldest first."""
    # debt: records are read through links (only writes refuse them); a planted record's fields reach
    # status and a waiter's stderr. Upgrade: O_NOFOLLOW reads, and skip a record not owned by its account.
    out = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir(HOLDERS)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return out
    for n in names:
        if not n.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(HOLDERS, n), encoding="utf-8") as f:
                h = json.load(f)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if not isinstance(h, dict) or not isinstance(h.get("pid"), int):
            continue
        # another account wrote it, maybe by hand or half-way: fill what status and the waiters print
        for k, v in (("account", "?"), ("kind", "?"), ("repo", "?"), ("command", ""), ("state", "?")):
            if not isinstance(h.get(k), str):
                h[k] = v
        for k in ("queued", "since", "cpus", "pgid", "observed"):  # sorted and subtracted: a string here crashed every run
            if k in h and (isinstance(h[k], bool) or not isinstance(h[k], (int, float))):
                h[k] = None if k != "queued" else 0
        if alive(h.get("pid", -1), h.get("start")):
            h["orphan"] = False  # from liveness only: a record can't claim it (an orphan's scope is followed on that word)
            out.append(h)
        elif h.get("pgid") and (group_alive(h["pgid"]) or scope_pids(scope_dir(h.get("cgroup"), h["pid"]))):
            # the wrapper was killed outright: its slot is gone, but its command still runs (maybe only a setsid
            # child in its scope, once the leader has exited)
            h["orphan"] = True
            if h.get("paused_since") or h.get("load_paused_since"):
                resume_orphan(h)
            out.append(h)
    return sorted(out, key=lambda h: h.get("queued", 0))


def resume_orphan(h):
    """A wrapper killed while the throttle had its run stopped: CONT the group and its scope, so the run never sits
    frozen without a slot. Safe to repeat (a running group ignores it), so every read that finds the orphan sends it."""
    try:
        os.killpg(h["pgid"], signal.SIGCONT)
    except OSError:
        pass  # gone, or another account's (its own status resumes it)
    signal_scope(holder_scope(h, False), signal.SIGCONT)


def heavy_lockers():
    """pid -> 'WRITE'|'READ' for every process holding a flock on the heavy file, from /proc/locks.
    This sees runs that never went through testslot (test-parallel on its own). Linux only: macOS
    has no lock table to read, so this finds nothing there; test_parallel_runs() still finds a test-parallel by ps."""
    # debt: on macOS status says "free" while another flock holder outside testslot (not a test-parallel,
    # which test_parallel_runs lists by name) holds the gate; admission still waits on it. Upgrade: lsof on
    # the heavy file, which is slow, so only when a run is waiting.
    if not LINUX:
        return {}
    try:
        st = os.stat(HEAVY)
        with open("/proc/locks", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            lines = f.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return {}
    # debt: matches on the inode number alone, since on btrfs stat's st_dev (the subvolume's anonymous
    # device) differs from the superblock device /proc/locks prints. Another filesystem's file with the
    # same inode number would be miscounted; upgrade: resolve the subvolume's device via its mount.
    ino = f":{st.st_ino}"
    found = {}
    for line in lines:
        parts = line.split()
        if "->" in parts:  # a blocked waiter, not a holder
            continue
        if len(parts) >= 6 and parts[1] == "FLOCK" and parts[5].endswith(ino):
            found[int(parts[4])] = parts[3]
    return found


WRAPPERS = {"env", "timeout", "nice", "nohup", "setsid", "caffeinate", "ionice", "taskset", "stdbuf", "time"}


def test_parallel_runs():
    """pid -> pgid of every running `tools/test-parallel` (Cat Herder's suite runner), whoever started it.
    Cat Herder starts it by itself (remote-test, a hand run), outside testslot, and it takes the heavy flock
    only when CAT_HERDER_TEST_SLOT points there, so heavy_lockers() may not see it. A
    worker that test-parallel forks is another program. Only the first test-parallel seen in each process
    group is kept, which is its parent while ps lists by ascending pid."""
    # debt: matches Cat Herder's runner by name; another suite runner outside testslot still goes unseen.
    # Upgrade: count CPU by process group against the holders' groups.
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["ps", "-Ao", "pid=,pgid=,args="], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", timeout=5).stdout
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return {}
    found = {}
    for line in out.splitlines():
        parts = line.split(None, 2)
        if len(parts) < 3 or not parts[0].isdigit() or not parts[1].lstrip("-").isdigit():
            continue
        # the runner itself, perhaps behind interpreters, wrappers and their flags (`python3 -u tools/test-parallel`,
        # `timeout 600 env X=1 tools/test-parallel`), but never as another program's argument (`pgrep -f test-parallel`)
        hit = False
        for a in parts[2].split()[:8]:
            if a == "test-parallel" or a.endswith("/test-parallel"):
                hit = True
                break
            if not (re.fullmatch(r"python[0-9.]*|bash|sh|zsh", os.path.basename(a), re.I) or os.path.basename(a) in WRAPPERS
                    or a.startswith("-") or re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?[smhd]?", a) or "=" in a):
                break
        if hit:
            pid, pgid = int(parts[0]), int(parts[1])
            if pgid not in found.values():
                found[pid] = pgid
    return found


def outside_runs(known):
    """(pid, how) for each run outside testslot: a flock on the heavy file, or a test-parallel whose
    group is not a holder's. known is the holders' pids and pgids."""
    seen, runs = set(), []
    for p in heavy_lockers():
        if p not in known:
            seen.add(p)
            runs.append((p, "flock"))
    for p, pg in test_parallel_runs().items():
        if p in known or pg in known or p in seen:
            continue
        runs.append((p, "test-parallel"))
    return runs


def proc_info(pid):
    """(account, cwd, command) for a pid, as far as this account may read it."""
    if not LINUX:
        def out(cmd):
            try:
                return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", timeout=5).stdout.strip()
            except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
                return ""
        cwd = next((l[1:] for l in out(["lsof", "-a", "-p", str(pid), "-d", "cwd", "-Fn"]).splitlines()
                    if l.startswith("n")), "?")
        return (out(["ps", "-o", "user=", "-p", str(pid)]) or "?", cwd,
                out(["ps", "-o", "command=", "-p", str(pid)]) or "?")
    try:
        import pwd
        uid = os.stat(f"/proc/{pid}").st_uid
        acct = pwd.getpwuid(uid).pw_name
    except (OSError, KeyError):
        acct = "?"
    try:
        cwd = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd")
    except OSError:
        cwd = "?"
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb") as f:
            cmd = " ".join(a.decode(errors="replace") for a in f.read().split(b"\0") if a)
    except OSError:
        cmd = "?"
    return acct, cwd, cmd


def outside_note():
    """What test-parallel wrote into the heavy file ("<cwd>, pid N, since T"), readable by both accounts,
    where /proc/<pid>/cwd of the other account is not."""
    try:
        with open(HEAVY, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            return f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def describe_holders(mine=None):
    """One line per current holder, for a waiting run to print."""
    hs = read_holders()
    lines = [f"{h.get('account')} {h.get('kind')} in {h.get('repo')}, {cpus_of(h)} cpus: {h.get('command')} (pgid {h.get('pgid')})"
             for h in hs if holds_cpus(h)]
    # a run queued earlier goes first, so it is what a later run waits behind too
    lines += [f"{h.get('account')} {h.get('kind')}, waiting ahead of you, in {h.get('repo')}, {cpus_of(h)} cpus: {h.get('command')}"
              for h in hs if h.get("state") == "waiting" and h["pid"] != os.getpid() and h.get("kind") != "track"
              and (mine is None or h.get("queued", 0) < mine) and not stopped(h["pid"])]
    known = {h["pid"] for h in hs} | {h.get("pgid") for h in hs if h.get("pgid")}
    for pid, how in outside_runs(known):
        acct, cwd, cmd = proc_info(pid)
        note = outside_note() if how == "flock" else ""
        lines.append(f"{acct} outside testslot in {cwd}: {cmd} (pid {pid})" + (f" [{note}]" if note else ""))
    return lines or ["another run"]


def repo_dir():
    try:
        top = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", timeout=5)
        if top.returncode == 0 and top.stdout.strip():
            return top.stdout.strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        pass
    return os.getcwd()


def write_holder(path, rec):
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.{time.time_ns()}.tmp"  # never another account's leftover
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o664)
    os.fchmod(fd, 0o664)  # O_EXCL made it, so it is ours
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(rec, f)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def own_mode(fd, mode):
    """chmod an open file only if this account owns it. Every account can replace files in the shared
    folder, so a name there may now be a link to someone's private file: never widen that file's mode."""
    try:
        if os.fstat(fd).st_uid == os.getuid():
            os.fchmod(fd, mode)
    except OSError:
        pass


def history_line(h, ended, code):
    """The history record for a finished run: plain fields, so another backend could write the same."""
    started = h.get("since")
    return {"pid": h.get("pid"), "pgid": h.get("pgid"), "account": h.get("account"), "repo": h.get("repo"),
            "command": (h.get("command") or "")[:500], "kind": h.get("kind"), "cpus": cpus_of(h),
            "queued": h.get("queued"), "started": started, "ended": ended, "exit": code,
            "wait_s": round(started - h["queued"], 3) if started and h.get("queued") else None,
            "run_s": round(ended - started, 3) if started and ended else None,
            "load_at_start": h.get("load_at_start"), "cancelled": bool(h.get("cancelled")), "log": h.get("log"),
            "backfill": bool(h.get("backfill")), "requeued": bool(h.get("requeued_from")),
            "stop_reason": h.get("stop_reason") if isinstance(h.get("stop_reason"), str) else None,
            "limit_s": h.get("limit_s") if isinstance(h.get("limit_s"), (int, float)) and not isinstance(h.get("limit_s"), bool) else None,
            "wait_reason": h.get("wait_reason") if isinstance(h.get("wait_reason"), str) else "",
            "requeue_peak": h.get("requeue_peak") if isinstance(h.get("requeue_peak"), (int, float)) and not isinstance(h.get("requeue_peak"), bool) else None,
            "pauses": h.get("pauses") or 0, "paused_s": round(h.get("paused_s") or 0, 2),
            "max_pause_s": round(h.get("max_pause_s") or 0, 2),
            "key": h.get("key"), "peak": h.get("peak") if isinstance(h.get("peak"), (int, float)) else None,
            "cpu_s": h.get("cpu_s") if isinstance(h.get("cpu_s"), (int, float)) and not isinstance(h.get("cpu_s"), bool) else None}


def append_history(rec):
    """Append one line to history.jsonl, rolling it to history.jsonl.1 past HISTORY_MAX.
    Never fails the run: history is a record, not a gate."""
    data = (json.dumps(rec) + "\n").encode()
    for _ in range(5):
        try:
            # O_NOFOLLOW: another account may have swapped the name for a link to a file of ours
            fd = os.open(HISTORY, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o664)
        except OSError:
            return
        own_mode(fd, 0o664)  # umask 022 would leave the creator's file shut to the other account
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            try:
                same = os.stat(HISTORY).st_ino == os.fstat(fd).st_ino
            except FileNotFoundError:
                same = False
            if not same:  # rolled over while we waited for the lock: open the new file
                continue
            if os.fstat(fd).st_size > HISTORY_MAX:
                os.replace(HISTORY, HISTORY + ".1")  # under the lock; the next pass opens a fresh file
                continue
            try:
                os.fchmod(fd, 0o664)  # the other account appends too; umask 022 drops the group bit
            except OSError:
                pass  # not ours to chmod: the creator already did
            os.write(fd, data)
            return
        except OSError:
            return
        finally:
            os.close(fd)


def prune(me):
    """Drop holder files of dead processes (an admitted one leaves a history line, marked lost unless
    it recorded its exit), and this account's logs older than LOG_DAYS."""
    try:
        for n in os.listdir(HOLDERS):
            p = os.path.join(HOLDERS, n)
            try:
                if n.endswith(".tmp") and time.time() - os.stat(p).st_mtime > 60:
                    os.unlink(p)
                elif n.endswith(".json") and n[:-5].isdigit():
                    with open(p, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                        h = json.load(f)
                    if alive(int(n[:-5]), h.get("start")):
                        continue
                    pg = h.get("pgid")
                    if not (pg and (group_alive(pg) or scope_pids(scope_dir(h.get("cgroup"), h.get("pid"))))):
                        os.unlink(p)  # whoever unlinks it writes its history, so it is written once
                        if h.get("since"):
                            line = history_line(h, h.get("ended") or time.time(), h.get("exit"))
                            line["lost"] = "exit" not in h
                            append_history(line)
            except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError, TypeError):
                pass
        cutoff = time.time() - LOG_DAYS * 86400
        for n in os.listdir(LOGS):
            p = os.path.join(LOGS, n)
            if n.startswith(me + "-"):
                try:
                    if os.stat(p).st_mtime < cutoff:
                        os.unlink(p)
                except OSError:
                    pass
    except OSError:
        pass


def waiters_ahead(hs, me, mine):
    """The waiting runs queued before the one at `mine`, in turn order."""
    return [h for h in hs if h.get("state") == "waiting" and h.get("kind") != "track" and h["pid"] != me
            and (h.get("queued", 0), h["pid"]) < (mine, me) and not stopped(h["pid"])]


def over_load_cap(cpus, load, load_cap):
    """Whether a run of `cpus` is held back by the load while anything holds: its cpus on top of the load go over the cap."""
    return load + cpus > load_cap


# A head that over_load_cap holds back can't start while anything holds (why_wait checks the same thing), so
# reserving for it at once would idle the box for nothing: smaller runs keep backfilling past it. To keep it from
# starving behind a stream of them, it reserves after this many times RESERVE, and then blocks every later run,
# fitting ones too, until the holders drain and it starts on the idle slot: that is the intended trade against
# starvation, not a bug. A --heavy run is the exception: it always takes the whole cap, so the plain RESERVE is
# how it drains the slot. Default 600 s x 6 = one hour (as of 2026-10-21).
IDLE_ONLY_RESERVE_X = 6


def reserve_moment(h, load, load_cap):
    """When the queue starts reserving cpus for the waiting run `h`: it queued + RESERVE (times IDLE_ONLY_RESERVE_X when the
    load holds it back while anything holds), or None for a run with no queue time."""
    if not h.get("queued"):
        return None
    idle_only = h.get("kind") != "heavy" and over_load_cap(cpus_of(h), load, load_cap)
    return h["queued"] + RESERVE * (IDLE_ONLY_RESERVE_X if idle_only else 1)


def reserving(waiting, now=None):
    """The waiting run the queue reserves cpus for: the first in turn order that has waited past RESERVE (past
    IDLE_ONLY_RESERVE_X times RESERVE when the load holds it back while anything holds), else None.
    `waiting` is in turn order (read_holders sorts by queue time)."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    load_cap, load = read_load_cap(), loadavg()[0]
    for h in waiting:
        at = reserve_moment(h, load, load_cap)
        if at is not None and now > at:
            return h
    return None


# The stable code of each reason a waiting run can be held for, recorded as its wait_reason. The text of a reason
# carries live numbers (elapsed time, load); the code never does, so it changes only when the reason does. Every
# reason goes through wait_why, which refuses a code not listed here.
WAIT_CODES = ("reserved", "queued-ahead", "cpus", "load", "gate", "no-limit")


def wait_why(code, text):
    """(code, text) for one reason; a code not in WAIT_CODES is a bug."""
    if code not in WAIT_CODES:
        raise ValueError(f"unlisted wait code {code!r}")
    return code, text


def why_wait(hs, me, mine, cpus, limit_s=None):
    """Why a run queued at `mine` asking for `cpus` (with a time limit of `limit_s` seconds, or none) can't start now, or None if it can."""
    held = wait_check(hs, me, mine, cpus, limit_s)
    return held[1] if held else None


def wait_check(hs, me, mine, cpus, limit_s=None):
    """(code, reason text) for why a run queued at `mine` asking for `cpus` can't start now, or None if it can.
    A run that would start past a waiting run (a head) needs a time limit within the backfill limit, and against a reserved head
    that limit must end within the backfill limit of the moment the head began to reserve."""
    # both caps are read on every check, not once at start: a cap raised or lowered while runs wait reaches them
    cap, load_cap = read_cap(), read_load_cap()
    ahead = waiters_ahead(hs, me, mine)
    backfill_s = read_backfill_limit() * 60
    head = reserving(ahead)
    if head:
        held = wait_why("reserved", f"cpus are reserved for the run at the head (pgid {head.get('pgid') or '-'}, {cpus_of(head)} cpus, "
                        f"waiting {fmt_elapsed(head['queued'])}); nothing passes it")
        if limit_s is None:
            return held
        # the earliest moment a head began to reserve: the run's limit must end by then plus the backfill limit
        now, load = time.time(), loadavg()[0]
        moments = [m for m in (reserve_moment(h, load, load_cap) for h in ahead) if m is not None and m < now]
        if not moments or now + limit_s > min(moments) + backfill_s:
            return held
    # backfill: only a run asking for more cpus than this one is passed over; a run of this size or smaller goes first
    first = [h for h in ahead if cpus_of(h) <= cpus]
    if first:
        return wait_why("queued-ahead", f"{len(first)} run(s) queued ahead of this one")
    if ahead and (limit_s is None or limit_s > backfill_s):
        h = ahead[0]
        why = "no time limit (--max-minutes)" if limit_s is None else f"a time limit over the backfill limit of {backfill_s / 60:g} minutes"
        return wait_why("no-limit", f"{why}: the run ahead (pid {h.get('pid')}, {cpus_of(h)} cpus) is still waiting")
    used = sum(charged_cpus(h) for h in hs if holds_cpus(h) and h["pid"] != me)
    if used + cpus > cap:
        return wait_why("cpus", f"cpus: {used:g} of {cap} in use, this run needs {cpus}")
    # debt: the 1-minute load lags. Just after a run ends it still counts that run for some seconds,
    # so the next start can wait until it falls under the cap; just after a start it doesn't count it
    # yet, which the charge check above covers. Upgrade: subtract the running runs' share, or
    # read /proc/stat. Ceiling: outside work that keeps the load at the cap holds every run, by design
    # (the agreed gate); the first run is checked against the load alone, not load plus its cpus, or a
    # --heavy run could start only on an idle box.
    load = loadavg()[0]
    if load >= load_cap:  # always, so outside work holds back even the first run
        return wait_why("load", f"load: {load:.1f} now, at or over the load cap {load_cap:g}")
    if used and over_load_cap(cpus, load, load_cap):
        return wait_why("load", f"load: {load:.1f} now, this run needs {cpus}, load cap {load_cap:g}")
    return None


TTY_STOPS = {signal.SIGTSTP, signal.SIGTTIN, signal.SIGTTOU}


def turnstile_held(turn):
    """Hold the turnstile for a moment, with Ctrl-Z held off, so a stopped run can't freeze every admission."""
    class Held:
        def __enter__(self):
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, TTY_STOPS)
            fcntl.flock(turn, fcntl.LOCK_EX)

        def __exit__(self, *_):
            fcntl.flock(turn, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, TTY_STOPS)
    return Held()


def take_slots(rec, holder_path, heavy):
    """Wait our turn and admit this run within the cpu cap and the load: first come among runs of a size, a run that
    fits may be backfilled ahead of a bigger waiter, and nothing passes a head that has waited past the reserve time.
    Each check holds the turnstile for a moment, so two runs can't both see the same free cpus.
    Returns (gate descriptors held by the wrapper, whether we waited)."""
    # debt: with ps down for good (macOS), every holder counts as alive, so a waiter waits on silently
    # and never gives up: fail closed, but quiet. Upgrade: say "can't tell, ps is failing" after a minute.
    # debt: on macOS a nested run is seen only through its parent chain, so one started by a
    # double-forked child (reparented to launchd) isn't nested and waits on its own outer run.
    global MY_QUEUED
    turn = open_lock(TURNSTILE)
    gate = open_lock(HEAVY)
    me = rec["pid"]
    said = None
    code = None
    try:
        # our place in line: stamped and made visible under the turnstile, so no later run is seen first
        with turnstile_held(turn):
            MY_QUEUED = rec["queued"] = time.time()
            if rec.get("head"):  # a requeued run goes ahead of everything waiting
                ahead = [h["queued"] for h in read_holders() if h.get("state") == "waiting" and h["pid"] != me
                         and isinstance(h.get("queued"), (int, float))]
                if ahead:
                    MY_QUEUED = rec["queued"] = min(MY_QUEUED, min(ahead) - 0.001)
            write_holder(holder_path, rec)
        while True:
            with turnstile_held(turn):
                held = wait_check(read_holders(), me, rec["queued"], cpus_of(rec), rec.get("limit_s"))
                reason = held[1] if held else None
                if held is None:
                    try:  # the old gate: a run outside testslot (test-parallel's LOCK_EX) holds it
                        fcntl.flock(gate, (fcntl.LOCK_EX if heavy else fcntl.LOCK_SH) | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                    except BlockingIOError:
                        held = wait_why("gate", "the test gate, held outside testslot or by a heavy run")
                        reason = held[1]
                if held is None:
                    # counted from here on, before the turnstile frees, so the next check sees our cpus
                    # rec only says admitted once the holder does: a cancel or a failed write before that
                    # leaves no history line for a run that never started
                    passed = waiters_ahead(read_holders(), me, rec["queued"])
                    # the holder file keeps the size the run declared; every view clamps it with cpus_of, by design
                    admitted = dict(rec, state="admitted", since=time.time(), load_at_start=loadavg()[0],
                                    backfill=bool(passed))
                    write_holder(holder_path, admitted)
                    rec.update(admitted)
                    return [gate], said is not None
                if held[0] != code:  # codes, not text: the text carries live numbers and changes every second
                    code = rec["wait_reason"] = held[0]
                    write_holder(holder_path, rec)
            if reason != said:
                head = "waiting" if said is None else "still waiting"
                print(f"testslot: {head}: {reason}; held by:", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
                for line in describe_holders(MY_QUEUED):
                    print(f"  {line}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
                said = reason
            time.sleep(1)
    finally:
        os.close(turn)


KEY_MAX = 200   # a longer key is stored as a hash: a caller naming 100 modules needn't care


def store_key(key):
    """The key as stored and compared: as given, or a hash of it past KEY_MAX characters."""
    key = " ".join(str(key).split())
    return key if len(key) <= KEY_MAX else "sha256:" + hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest()


def derive_key(command):
    """A key for a command with no --key: the program by its base name and the arguments with some of what changes
    from run to run taken out: a bare path argument to its base name, hex runs of 8 or more and numbers of 5 or more
    digits to a mark, --log and its value dropped. So two runs of one command from two temp trees agree when the trees
    differ only in those. Not covered: a path inside a dash token (--junit=/tmp/x/out.xml, -o/tmp/x/out keep the
    whole path), a random temp name (mktemp's tmp.Ab12Cd), and two scripts with the same base name in different
    folders (python3 a/run.py and python3 b/run.py are one key). debt: a heuristic; a caller that knows what its
    command is (Cat Herder's remote-test) passes --key instead, which is the way."""
    out, skip = [], False
    for tok in command:
        if skip:
            skip = False
            continue
        if tok == "--log":
            skip = True
            continue
        if tok.startswith("--log="):
            continue
        if "/" in tok and not tok.startswith("-"):
            tok = os.path.basename(tok.rstrip("/")) or tok
        tok = re.sub(r"[0-9a-f]{8,}", "<h>", tok)
        tok = re.sub(r"\d{5,}", "<n>", tok)
        out.append(tok)
    return store_key(" ".join(out))


def cmd_suggest(argv):
    """Print the last recorded peak cpu for a key as a whole number, or --default N, or nothing. The latest
    finished, non-cancelled light run with that key and a usable reading wins (not the highest), and a key with no
    such run in the two history generations gives the default. A suggestion above the cap is clamped by `run --cpus`,
    which says so on the caller's stderr. Runs of kind track (their reading is waiting on an API) and heavy (the whole
    box by definition) are never recorded or offered."""
    # debt: reads both history files in full on every call (up to 2 x HISTORY_MAX, so some tens of ms); upgrade:
    # keep a small per-key index file, or read the files backwards and stop at the first match.
    key, default = None, None
    while argv:
        a = argv.pop(0)
        if a == "--key" and argv:
            key = store_key(argv.pop(0))
        elif a == "--default" and argv:
            n = argv.pop(0)
            if not n.isdigit() or int(n) < 1:
                die(f"suggest: --default wants a whole number of 1 or more, not {n}")
            default = int(n)
        elif a == "--":
            if not argv:
                die("usage: testslot suggest [--default N] (--key KEY | -- <command...>)")
            key = derive_key(argv)
            break
        else:
            die(f"suggest: unknown option {a}")
    if key is None:
        die("usage: testslot suggest [--default N] (--key KEY | -- <command...>)")
    peak = None
    try:  # a record, not a gate: whatever goes wrong reading it, the caller gets its default or nothing
        for r in read_history(0):
            p = r.get("peak")
            if (r.get("key") == key and r.get("kind") not in ("track", "heavy") and not r.get("cancelled")
                    and isinstance(p, (int, float))
                    and not isinstance(p, bool) and math.isfinite(p) and p >= 0):
                peak = p   # oldest first, so the last one that fits is the latest run
    except Exception:
        peak = None
    if peak is not None:
        print(max(1, math.ceil(peak)))
    elif default is not None:
        print(default)
    return 0


def clock_seconds(text):
    """ps's cumulative cpu time, [[dd-]hh:]mm:ss[.ss], in seconds; None if it isn't that."""
    try:
        days, _, rest = text.rpartition("-")
        secs = 0.0
        for part in rest.split(":"):
            secs = secs * 60 + float(part)
        return secs + (float(days) * 86400 if days else 0)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def group_cpu_seconds(pgid, seen):
    """Cpu seconds used so far by the processes that have been in a process group (user + system), or None if
    the processes can't be listed. `seen` maps pid to the most cpu time that pid has shown, and is kept by the
    caller between calls: a process that has exited drops out of the listing, but its cpu stays in the total.
    (Counting reaped children through their parent would count that cpu twice.) Limit: a process that starts and
    exits between two samples is never seen, so its cpu is never counted; the sampler is after what holds a slot
    (long-lived processes such as a python at 400%), and a run of many short-lived children reads low.
    This is the fallback and the macOS path; a Linux run in a cgroup is read by cgroup_cpu_seconds, which has
    neither gap and is not escaped by a descendant that calls setsid.
    debt: the dict holds a float per process it observes, and a pid reused inside the group keeps the larger
    of the two readings. Upgrade: none planned, since Linux has the cgroup.
    Linux: /proc stat fields. macOS: ps's cumulative time, not %cpu, which there is a lifetime average.
    Never raises, and a ps that hangs is killed after 2 s."""
    if LINUX:
        try:
            names = os.listdir("/proc")
        except OSError:
            return None
        ticks = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")
        for n in names:
            if not n.isdigit():
                continue
            try:
                with open(f"/proc/{n}/stat", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                    fields = f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()
                if int(fields[2]) == pgid:
                    seen[int(n)] = max(seen.get(int(n), 0.0), (int(fields[11]) + int(fields[12])) / ticks)  # utime stime
            except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError):
                continue  # gone since the listing
    else:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-Ao", "pid=,pgid=,time="], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", timeout=2,
                               env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL="C"))
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            return None
        if r.returncode != 0:
            return None
        for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
            f = line.split()
            if len(f) == 3 and f[0].isdigit() and f[1] == str(pgid) and clock_seconds(f[2]) is not None:
                seen[int(f[0])] = max(seen.get(int(f[0]), 0.0), clock_seconds(f[2]))
    return sum(seen.values()) if seen else None


def scope_dir(path, pid):
    """The cgroup directory a holder record names, or None unless it is this wrapper's own testslot scope under the
    cgroup root: its name is testslot-<wrapper pid>-<time>.scope. A record is group-writable, so what it names is
    checked before anything is read or signalled, and one forged to name another run's scope is not followed."""
    if not isinstance(path, str) or not path.startswith(CGROUP_ROOT + "/") or isinstance(pid, bool) or not isinstance(pid, int):
        return None
    real = os.path.realpath(path)
    name = os.path.basename(real)
    ok = real == path and name.startswith(f"testslot-{pid}-") and name.endswith(".scope")
    return real if ok else None


def proc_cgroup(pid):
    """The cgroup path /proc says a process is in (as it appears after the root), or None."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cgroup", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith("0::"):
                    return line.strip()[3:]
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None


def parent_of(pid):
    """The parent pid of a process, or None when it is gone."""
    try:
        if LINUX:
            with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                return int(f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[1])
        p = ps_table().get(pid)
        return p[1] if p else None
    except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError, PsFailed):
        return None


def holder_scope(h, genuine):
    """The cgroup directory of a holder's run that may be followed (read or signalled), or None. A record is
    group-writable, so one forged to name another run's scope must not be followed. The scope must carry the
    holder's pid in its name; when the group's leader exists it must be in that very cgroup, and its record must be
    the genuine one (its wrapper the leader's parent) unless the wrapper is gone (an orphan). With the leader gone
    only an orphan's scope is followed (its name is all there is to check)."""
    d = scope_dir(h.get("cgroup"), h.get("pid"))
    if not d:
        return None
    pg = h.get("pgid")
    lead = proc_cgroup(pg) if pg else None
    if lead is not None:
        return d if lead == d[len(CGROUP_ROOT):] and (genuine or h.get("orphan")) else None
    return d if h.get("orphan") else None


def scope_pids(d):
    """The pids in a cgroup, [] when it is gone (a scope vanishes when its last process does) or unreadable."""
    if not d:
        return []
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(d, "cgroup.procs"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            return [int(x) for x in f.read().split()]
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return []


def signal_scope(d, sig):
    """Send sig to every process in the cgroup (a child that called setsid is in no group of the run's)."""
    for p in scope_pids(d):
        try:
            os.kill(p, sig)
        except OSError:
            pass


def kill_scope(d):
    """KILL everything in the cgroup: cgroup.kill (Linux 5.14+) in one go, else signal what cgroup.procs lists,
    again until it is empty (a process can fork while the list is read)."""
    if not d:
        return
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(d, "cgroup.kill"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("1")
        return
    except OSError:
        pass
    for _ in range(5):
        if not scope_pids(d):
            return
        signal_scope(d, signal.SIGKILL)
        time.sleep(0.05)


def cgroup_cpu_seconds(d):
    """Cpu seconds the cgroup has used (cpu.stat usage_usec: user + system, every process it ever held, exited
    ones included), or None when it can't be read."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(d, "cpu.stat"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                key, _, val = line.partition(" ")
                if key == "usage_usec":
                    return int(val) / 1e6
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    return None


def scope_command(command, unit):
    """(argv to run the command in a user scope named unit, None, extra env), or (command, why not, {}) when no scope can
    be made: forced off, no cgroup v2, or a command too long for the environment. A launch that then fails is caught
    by the caller (no marker byte), not probed for here: a probe would cost a second scope inside the slot on every
    run. A command that isn't found goes straight to Popen, which reports it as 127 as it always did. The command travels in the environment and a python one-liner execs it:
    systemd-run rewrites its arguments (a `$$` becomes `$`), and the command must run exactly as typed."""
    if NO_CGROUP:
        return command, "TESTSLOT_NO_CGROUP is set", {}
    if not LINUX:
        return command, None, {}
    if shutil.which(command[0], path=os.environ.get("PATH")) is None:
        return command, None, {}
    if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(CGROUP_ROOT, "cgroup.controllers")):
        return command, "no cgroup v2 here", {}
    packed = json.dumps(command)
    if len(packed) > 100_000:  # one environment string is capped at 128 KiB (MAX_ARG_STRLEN)
        return command, "the command is too long to pass through the environment", {}
    base = ["systemd-run", "--user", "--scope", "--quiet", "--collect"]
    # The byte on TESTSLOT_MARKFD tells the wrapper the scope started and the command is about to run. The write is
    # not guarded: if the byte can't be written the command must not run, and the wrapper, seeing the pipe end with no
    # byte, runs it once on the group path. Closing the pipe is guarded (it only matters after the byte is sent).
    prog = ("import os,json\nfd = int(os.environ.pop('TESTSLOT_MARKFD'))\nos.write(fd, b'1')\n"
            "try:\n os.close(fd)\nexcept OSError:\n pass\n"
            "a = json.loads(os.environ.pop('TESTSLOT_ARGV'))\nos.execvp(a[0], a)")
    return base + ["--unit", unit, "--", sys.executable, "-c", prog], None, {"TESTSLOT_ARGV": packed}


def find_scope(proc, unit, owner):
    """(the cgroup directory of the scope proc has moved into (named for wrapper `owner`), None), or (None, why not):
    it polls up to 3 s for a scope that exists with a cpu.stat that reads. A command that ends before the scope is
    seen ends the poll at once (it has exited); one that lives on without a scope costs the 3 s."""
    pid = proc.pid
    want = "/" + unit + ".scope"
    end = time.monotonic() + 3
    why = "its scope was not found (the command may have ended first)"
    while True:
        try:
            with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cgroup", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                for line in f:
                    path = line.strip().partition("::")[2]
                    if line.startswith("0::") and path.endswith(want):
                        d = scope_dir(CGROUP_ROOT + path, owner)
                        if d and os.path.isdir(d):
                            if cgroup_cpu_seconds(d) is not None:
                                return d, None  # a directory we can read cpu from; else keep polling, then fall back
                            why = "its cpu.stat is not readable"
        except OSError:
            return None, why
        if time.monotonic() > end or proc.poll() is not None:
            return None, why
        time.sleep(0.01)


def read_marker(fd, seconds):
    """What the launcher's pipe says within `seconds`: "byte" (the scope started and the command is about to run),
    "eof" (every writer is gone and no byte came, so the command never ran), or "timeout" (neither yet: unknown).
    The read end must stay open until a byte or the end of the file, or the one-liner's write would break."""
    end = time.monotonic() + seconds
    while True:
        try:
            return "byte" if os.read(fd, 1) == b"1" else "eof"
        except BlockingIOError:
            pass
        except OSError:
            return "eof"
        if time.monotonic() > end:
            return "timeout"
        time.sleep(0.01)


def fake_observed(pgid=None):
    """The reading tests put in TESTSLOT_OBSERVED_DIR/<pgid> (this run's own), else in TESTSLOT_OBSERVED, or None."""
    try:
        if OBSERVED_DIR and pgid is not None:
            own = _read(os.path.join(OBSERVED_DIR, str(pgid)))
            if own is not None and own.strip():
                return float(own.strip())
        return float((_read(OBSERVED_FILE) or "").strip()) if OBSERVED_FILE else None
    except ValueError:
        return None


def start_sampler(rec, holder_path, pgid, lock, scope=None):
    """Sample this run's cgroup (`scope`), else its group, every SAMPLE_S in a thread of the wrapper and record it in the holder (`observed`,
    cores, over the last interval; no further averaging). The thread never touches the turnstile, only this
    run's own file, and `stop` ends it: set it under `lock` before the wrapper's last write, so the thread
    can't put a record back after the run has removed it. Returns (stop event, thread)."""
    # debt: one interval is the window, so a one-tick burst can hold the next start back for a few seconds;
    # upgrade: average the last two intervals. Processes that start and exit between samples go uncounted on the
    # process-group path (see group_cpu_seconds).
    stop = threading.Event()

    def loop():
        pids = {}
        before, at = (cgroup_cpu_seconds(scope) if scope else group_cpu_seconds(pgid, pids)), time.monotonic()
        low_since = None  # when the reading first fell under the declaration, and stayed there (monotonic)
        while not stop.wait(SAMPLE_S):
            seen = fake_observed(pgid) if (OBSERVED_FILE or OBSERVED_DIR) else None
            now = time.monotonic()
            total = None  # cpu seconds the run has used so far, for the history line (the tests check it against the real cost)
            if not (OBSERVED_FILE or OBSERVED_DIR):
                cpu = cgroup_cpu_seconds(scope) if scope else group_cpu_seconds(pgid, pids)
                total = None if cpu is None else round(cpu, 2)
                if cpu is not None and before is not None and now > at:
                    seen = max(0.0, (cpu - before) / (now - at))
                before, at = cpu, now
            seen = None if seen is None else round(seen, 2)
            with lock:
                if stop.is_set():
                    return
                peak = rec.get("peak")
                declared = cpus_of(rec)
                if pausing(rec):
                    shrink = bool(rec.get("shrunk"))  # a paused sample's near-zero cpu moves the shrink clock neither way
                elif (rec.get("observed_via") == "cgroup" and rec.get("kind") == "light" and seen is not None
                        and valid_observed(dict(rec, observed=seen)) is not None and seen < declared):
                    low_since = now if low_since is None else low_since
                    shrink = now - low_since >= SHRINK_AFTER_S
                else:
                    low_since, shrink = None, False  # a bad read, a climb back or a group reading restarts the clock
                if (rec.get("kind") == "light" and seen is not None
                        and valid_observed(dict(rec, observed=seen)) is not None and seen > (peak or 0)):
                    peak = seen   # the highest usable reading so far, for the history line; None readings count for nothing
                if (rec.get("observed") == seen and rec.get("peak") == peak and rec.get("cpu_s") == total
                        and bool(rec.get("shrunk")) == shrink and not shrink):  # a shrunk record is rewritten every sample
                    continue
                rec["shrunk"] = shrink
                rec["sampled_at"] = time.time() if shrink else None
                rec["sample_s"] = SAMPLE_S
                rec["shrink_after"] = SHRINK_AFTER_S  # N and the margin as this run's wrapper reads them, for status and the charge
                rec["shrink_margin"] = SHRINK_MARGIN
                rec["observed"] = seen  # None when this read failed: the run is charged what it declared
                rec["peak"] = peak
                rec["cpu_s"] = total
                try:
                    write_holder(holder_path, rec)
                except OSError:
                    pass

    thread = threading.Thread(target=loop, daemon=True)
    mask = {signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGALRM, signal.SIGUSR1, signal.SIGUSR2}
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, mask)  # a new thread inherits the mask: signals go to the main thread
    try:
        thread.start()
    finally:
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, mask)
    return stop, thread


# Load pause: while the 1-minute load is over LOAD_TRIGGER_X times the cores, the one run
# using the most above what it declared pauses (SIGSTOP), and resumes once the load is under the load cap.
LOAD_TRIGGER_X = 1.5


def worst_over_user(hs):
    """The holder to pause when the load is too high, or None. Light runs that are running, with a usable reading above
    what they declared, and not already paused by the load pause; the largest over-use in cpus wins, a tie going to the
    one started last. None when this would leave fewer than two runs going: a lone run is never paused."""
    live = [h for h in hs if h.get("state") in ("admitted", "running") and h.get("kind") != "track" and not h.get("orphan")]
    going = [h for h in live if not pausing(h)]
    if len(going) < 2:
        return None
    best = None
    for h in going:
        seen = valid_observed(h)
        if h.get("kind") != "light" or seen is None:
            continue
        over = round(seen - cpus_of(h), 2)
        if over <= 0:
            continue
        key = (over, h.get("since") or h.get("queued") or 0, h["pid"])
        if best is None or key > best[0]:
            best = (key, h)
    return best[1] if best else None


# Requeue, the last resort after the load pause: a light run whose unpaused reading is over REQUEUE_X
# times what it declared, and has been paused by the load pause for REQUEUE_AFTER_S (counted from its first load pause since it
# went over), with the load still over the load trigger and another run going, is stopped as a cancel and started again once,
# at the head of the queue, declaring its measured peak. The clock starts at the pause, never earlier: the pause must have had its turn.
REQUEUE_X = 2.0
try:
    REQUEUE_AFTER_S = float(os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_REQUEUE_AFTER_S") or 300)
    REQUEUE_AFTER_S = min(86400.0, max(1.0, REQUEUE_AFTER_S)) if math.isfinite(REQUEUE_AFTER_S) else 300.0  # tests set it small
except ValueError:
    REQUEUE_AFTER_S = 300.0


def requeue_size(peak):
    """What the run declares when it starts again: its peak, rounded up, held to the cap as it is now."""
    return int(min(max(1, math.ceil(peak)), max(1, read_cap())))


def requeue_due(rec, others_going, load, since_pause):
    """Whether this run is to be stopped and started again: a light run still reading over REQUEUE_X times its declared cpus
    that was first paused by the load pause since_pause seconds ago (None: it has not been paused since it went over, or it
    is not over), with the load over the trigger and another run going. Never a heavy, track or already requeued run, and
    never one with no usable reading (it is not over then)."""
    return (rec.get("kind") == "light" and not rec.get("requeued_from") and others_going >= 1 and since_pause is not None
            and since_pause >= REQUEUE_AFTER_S and load > LOAD_TRIGGER_X * CORES)


def start_throttle(rec, holder_path, pgid, lock, scope, is_cancelled, stop, ask_requeue=None, ask_stop=None):
    """The start-rate throttle, a second thread of the wrapper: every START_RATE_S it reads the host's process starts.
    Over the cap (averaged over START_RATE_WINDOW readings, with the latest over 80% of it), it stops the run's group (and its cgroup) and watches the
    1-second reading. A fall of half the cap within PAUSE_MAX_S makes the burst the run's own: it resumes once the
    reading is under 80% of the cap, or after PAUSE_MAX_S, and may pause again from the next reading. No such fall:
    the burst is outside work, so the run resumes and isn't paused again for HOLD_OFF_S. `stop` ends the thread
    (the cpu sampler's event, set under `lock`); the thread resumes the group before it ends.
    A cancel (`is_cancelled`) is checked before each SIGSTOP and again right after, since the wrapper's signal handler
    can't take `lock`: a TERM must never sit pending behind a stop."""
    # debt: on macOS a pause reaches the process group only, so a child that called setsid keeps running (Linux
    # stops the cgroup too). Upgrade: walk the run's descendants by ppid and stop them as well.
    rate = StartRate()

    def signal_run(sig):
        try:
            os.killpg(pgid, sig)
        except OSError:
            pass
        signal_scope(scope, sig)

    def say(text):
        try:
            print(f"testslot: {text}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass

    def loop():
        window = []
        paused_at = None      # monotonic, while paused
        base = None           # the averaged rate that tripped this pause
        own = False           # this pause saw the rate fall: the burst is the run's
        hold_until = 0.0
        last_line, since_line, paused_since_line = None, 0, 0.0
        load_at = None        # monotonic, while the load pause holds this run
        over_since = None     # monotonic: when the run's unpaused reading went over REQUEUE_X times its declaration (None: it is not over)
        lp_at_over = 0        # the load pauses counted when it did
        paused_first = None   # monotonic: when the load pause first held it since it went over: the requeue clock starts here
        asked = False         # the requeue was asked for: it is asked once

        def resume(now):
            nonlocal paused_at
            if load_at is None:  # a load pause still holds the run: it is resumed when both have cleared
                signal_run(signal.SIGCONT)
            took = now - paused_at
            paused_at = None
            with lock:
                rec["paused_since"] = None
                rec["resumed_at"] = time.time()
                rec["paused_s"] = (rec.get("paused_s") or 0) + took
                rec["max_pause_s"] = max(rec.get("max_pause_s") or 0, took)
                write()
            return took

        def write():
            if stop.is_set():
                return  # the run has removed its record, or is about to
            try:
                write_holder(holder_path, rec)
            except OSError:
                pass

        def load_tick(now):
            """The load pause: this wrapper pauses its own run when it is the worst over-user, and resumes it when the
            load is under the load cap. Every wrapper reads the same records, so exactly one decides it is the one."""
            nonlocal load_at
            load = loadavg()[0]
            if load_at is not None:
                if load < read_load_cap() or is_cancelled():
                    took = now - load_at
                    load_at = None
                    with lock:
                        rec["load_paused_since"] = None
                        rec["resumed_at"] = time.time()
                        rec["load_paused_s"] = (rec.get("load_paused_s") or 0) + took
                        write()
                    if paused_at is None:  # a start-rate pause still holds it otherwise
                        signal_run(signal.SIGCONT)
                    say(f"resumed after {took:.0f}s: load {load:g} is under the load cap {read_load_cap():g}")
                return
            if load <= LOAD_TRIGGER_X * CORES or is_cancelled():
                return
            target = worst_over_user(read_holders())
            if target is None or target.get("pid") != os.getpid():
                return
            seen = valid_observed(target)
            signal_run(signal.SIGSTOP)
            if is_cancelled():
                signal_run(signal.SIGCONT)
                return
            load_at = now
            with lock:
                rec["load_paused_since"] = time.time()
                rec["load_pauses"] = (rec.get("load_pauses") or 0) + 1
                write()
            say(f"paused: load {load:g} on {CORES} cores is over {LOAD_TRIGGER_X:g} times the cores, and this run uses "
                f"{seen:g} cpus but declared {cpus_of(rec):g}; it resumes when the load is under {read_load_cap():g}")

        def requeue_tick(now):
            """Ask the wrapper to stop this run and start it again, when requeue_due() says so. The 5 minutes count from the
            run's first load pause since its reading went over, not from when it went over, so the pause always has its turn
            first. A paused sample says nothing about what the run uses: the latest unpaused reading stands while it is paused."""
            nonlocal over_since, lp_at_over, paused_first, asked
            if ask_requeue is None or asked or is_cancelled():
                return
            with lock:
                if not pausing(rec):
                    seen = valid_observed(rec)
                    if seen is not None and seen > REQUEUE_X * cpus_of(rec):
                        if over_since is None:
                            over_since, lp_at_over, paused_first = now, rec.get("load_pauses") or 0, None
                    else:
                        over_since = paused_first = None
                if over_since is not None and paused_first is None and (rec.get("load_pauses") or 0) > lp_at_over:
                    paused_first = now
            if paused_first is None or now - paused_first < REQUEUE_AFTER_S:
                return
            load = loadavg()[0]
            if load <= LOAD_TRIGGER_X * CORES:
                return
            others = [h for h in read_holders() if h["pid"] != os.getpid() and h.get("state") in ("admitted", "running")
                      and h.get("kind") != "track" and not h.get("orphan")]
            if not requeue_due(rec, len(others), load, now - paused_first):
                return
            asked = True
            with lock:
                rec["requeue_peak"] = rec.get("peak")
                rec["requeue_declared"] = cpus_of(rec)
                rec["requeue_load"] = load
                write()
            ask_requeue()

        limit_asked = False

        def limit_tick():
            """Ask the wrapper to stop this run once its time limit has passed, when it started past a waiting run and a run
            that was ahead of it still waits. Its own check each tick, for every run with a limit (a heavy or requeued one too)."""
            nonlocal limit_asked
            # debt: checked once per START_RATE_S tick, so a stop lands up to a tick after the limit; the limit is minutes, so a tick is
            # noise. Upgrade: a timer set from the limit when the run starts.
            if ask_stop is None or limit_asked or is_cancelled():
                return
            limit, since = rec.get("limit_s"), rec.get("since")
            if not rec.get("backfill") or not limit or not since or time.time() - since <= limit:
                return
            if not waiters_ahead(read_holders(), os.getpid(), rec.get("queued", 0)):
                return
            limit_asked = True
            ask_stop()

        try:
            while not stop.wait(START_RATE_S):
                limit_tick()
                requeue_tick(time.monotonic())
                load_tick(time.monotonic())
                cap = read_start_rate_cap()
                if cap is None and paused_at is None:
                    window.clear()
                    continue
                r = rate.read()
                now = time.monotonic()
                if r is not None:
                    window = (window + [r])[-START_RATE_WINDOW:]
                    with lock:  # for status: one small write a second, and only while a cap is set
                        rec["host_rate"], rec["host_rate_at"] = round(r, 1), time.time()
                        write()
                if paused_at is not None:
                    if cap is None:  # the cap was taken away mid-pause
                        resume(now)
                        continue
                    if r is not None and base - r >= cap / 2:
                        own = True
                    if own and (r is not None and r < 0.8 * cap or now - paused_at >= PAUSE_MAX_S):
                        resume(now)
                    elif now - paused_at >= PAUSE_MAX_S:
                        resume(now)
                        hold_until = now + HOLD_OFF_S
                        say(f"host starts {base:.0f}/s did not fall while this run was paused, so they are not its own; "
                            f"not pausing it for {HOLD_OFF_S:g}s (cap {cap:g}/s)")
                    continue
                if cap is None or not window or now < hold_until or is_cancelled():
                    continue
                avg = sum(window) / len(window)
                # the latest reading must be over the resume line too: just after a resume the average still holds the
                # pre-pause readings, and pausing a run whose rate has already fallen would only flap
                if avg <= cap or r is None or r < 0.8 * cap:
                    continue
                signal_run(signal.SIGSTOP)
                if is_cancelled():  # a cancel began between the check and the stop: let its TERM through at once
                    signal_run(signal.SIGCONT)
                    continue
                paused_at, base, own = now, avg, False
                with lock:
                    rec["paused_since"] = time.time()
                    rec["pauses"] = (rec.get("pauses") or 0) + 1
                    write()
                since_line += 1
                if last_line is None:
                    say(f"paused (up to {PAUSE_MAX_S:g}s): host starts {avg:.0f}/s, cap {cap:g}/s")
                    last_line, since_line, paused_since_line = now, 0, rec.get("paused_s") or 0
                elif now - last_line >= PAUSE_REPORT_S:
                    took = (rec.get("paused_s") or 0) - paused_since_line
                    say(f"paused {since_line} times in {now - last_line:.0f}s ({took:.0f}s), host starts {avg:.0f}/s, cap {cap:g}/s")
                    last_line, since_line, paused_since_line = now, 0, rec.get("paused_s") or 0
        finally:
            if load_at is not None:  # likewise for the load pause
                signal_run(signal.SIGCONT)
                with lock:
                    rec["load_paused_since"] = None
            if paused_at is not None:  # however the thread ends, the run is left running
                signal_run(signal.SIGCONT)
                with lock:
                    took = time.monotonic() - paused_at
                    rec["paused_since"] = None
                    rec["paused_s"] = (rec.get("paused_s") or 0) + took
                    rec["max_pause_s"] = max(rec.get("max_pause_s") or 0, took)

    thread = threading.Thread(target=loop, daemon=True)
    mask = {signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGALRM, signal.SIGUSR1, signal.SIGUSR2}
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, mask)  # signals go to the main thread, as for the sampler
    try:
        thread.start()
    finally:
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, mask)
    return thread


def stop_sampler(sampler):
    """End the run's sampler: the flag is set under the lock, so no write follows it."""
    if sampler:
        stop, thread, lock, throttle = sampler
        with lock:
            stop.set()
        thread.join(timeout=3)
        throttle.join(timeout=3)  # it resumes a stopped group on its way out


def cmd_run(argv):
    heavy, track, log, cpus, key = False, False, None, None, None
    requeued = None  # internal: (declared, peak) of the run this one replaces, set by the wrapper that requeued it
    limit_s = None  # --max-minutes, in seconds
    while argv and argv[0] != "--":
        a = argv.pop(0)
        if a == "--requeued" and argv:
            try:
                d, _, pk = argv.pop(0).partition(":")
                requeued = (float(d), float(pk))
            except ValueError:
                die("run: --requeued is for testslot's own restart (DECLARED:PEAK)")
        elif a == "--heavy":
            heavy = True
        elif a == "--track":
            track = True
        elif a == "--log" and argv:
            log = argv.pop(0)
        elif a == "--key" and argv:
            key = argv.pop(0)
        elif a == "--max-minutes" and argv:
            n = argv.pop(0)
            try:
                limit_s = round(float(n) * 60, 3)
                if not math.isfinite(limit_s) or limit_s <= 0:
                    raise ValueError(n)
            except ValueError:
                die(f"run: --max-minutes wants a number of minutes above 0 (decimals are fine), not {n}")
        elif a == "--cpus" and argv:
            n = argv.pop(0)
            if not n.isdigit() or int(n) < 1:
                die(f"run: --cpus wants a whole number of 1 or more, not {n}")
            cpus = int(n)
        else:
            die(f"run: unknown option {a}")
    if not argv or argv[0] != "--" or len(argv) < 2:
        die("usage: testslot run [--cpus N | --heavy | --track] [--max-minutes N] [--key KEY] [--log FILE] -- <command...>")
    if heavy + track + (cpus is not None) > 1:
        die("run: --cpus, --heavy and --track exclude each other")
    command = argv[1:]
    kind = "heavy" if heavy else "track" if track else "light"
    if cpus and cpus > read_cap():
        print(f"testslot: --cpus {cpus} is over the cap; this run gets all {read_cap()}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    # stored as asked (heavy as none, which counts as the whole cap): the cap is applied at each check, so a
    # raise reaches a run that is already queued
    cpus = 0 if track else None if heavy else (cpus or 1)

    # Nested under a run that already holds a slot (testslot's or test-parallel's): waiting again
    # would wait on ourselves. An ancestor holding the gate in /proc/locks is the proof.
    outer = held_by_ancestor()
    if outer == "heavy" or (outer == "light" and not heavy):
        try:
            os.execvp(command[0], command)
        except OSError as e:
            die(f"run: {command[0]}: {e.strerror}", 127)
    if outer == "light" and heavy:
        die("run: a heavy run can't start inside a light one (it would wait on its own parent); "
            "run the outer command with --heavy", 1)
    if not os.path.isdir(HOLDERS):
        die(f"{DIR} is not set up: make it with its holders/ and logs/ folders "
            f'(mkdir -p "{DIR}/holders" "{DIR}/logs"), or set TESTSLOT_DIR to a folder you own and make holders/ and logs/ inside it; '
            "to share one gate between accounts, see the README's sharing steps (group, chmod -R g+rws)")

    me = getpass.getuser()
    prune(me)
    pid = os.getpid()
    stamp = datetime.datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    log = os.path.abspath(log) if log else os.path.join(LOGS, f"{me}-{stamp}-{pid}.log")
    holder_path = os.path.join(HOLDERS, f"{pid}.json")
    global MY_QUEUED
    MY_QUEUED = time.time()
    rec = {"pid": pid, "account": me, "repo": repo_dir(), "command": " ".join(command), "kind": kind, "limit_s": limit_s,
           "cpus": cpus, "key": store_key(key) if key else derive_key(command), "start": (proc_stat(pid) or (None, None))[1], "state": "waiting", "queued": MY_QUEUED, "since": None, "pgid": None, "log": log}
    if requeued:  # started again by its own wrapper: at the head of the queue, once, with its measured size
        rec.update(requeued_from={"declared": requeued[0], "peak": requeued[1], "cpus": cpus}, head=True)
    proc = None
    sampler = None  # (stop event, thread, lock) once the command runs

    cancelled = []
    ended = False
    result = None
    scope = None  # the run's cgroup directory once it is made, else None (the group path)

    def run_alive(pgid):
        """Whether anything of the run is left: its group, or its cgroup (which holds the setsid children too)."""
        return group_alive(pgid) or bool(scope_pids(scope))

    def end_group(pgid):
        """CONT then TERM the group and the cgroup, then KILL whatever is left after 5s. The CONT first: a group the
        throttle stopped would hold the TERM pending and be KILLed, recorded as a failure, not a cancel."""
        # OSError, not just ProcessLookupError: a setuid member makes killpg fail with EPERM
        for sig in (signal.SIGCONT, signal.SIGTERM):
            try:
                os.killpg(pgid, sig)
            except OSError:
                pass
            signal_scope(scope, sig)
        if not run_alive(pgid):
            return
        for _ in range(10):
            time.sleep(0.5)
            if not run_alive(pgid):
                return
        kill_group(pgid)

    def kill_group(pgid):
        if group_alive(pgid):  # re-checked, so a pgid already gone is never signalled
            try:
                os.killpg(pgid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                pass
        kill_scope(scope)

    def escalate(_signum, _frame):
        # SIGALRM: forward()'s 5 s timer, or `testslot stop --kill`, which leaves the KILL to this handler so the cancel
        # is recorded before the leader dies. A KILL from outside first would leave a leader that has ended, unreaped,
        # and forward() would take that for the command ending by itself (exit 137, counted as failed).
        if proc is not None:
            if not cancelled and not leader_ended():
                cancelled.append(signal.SIGTERM)
            kill_group(proc.pid)

    def leader_ended():
        """True once the command's leader has exited, reaped or not. proc.returncode is set only by the
        wrapper's wait(), so a stop in the gap between the exit and the reap would count as a cancel.
        waitid with WNOWAIT looks without reaping, so wait() still gets the code (no Popen.poll here: the
        handler runs inside wait(), which holds Popen's lock). A reaped child (ECHILD) has ended too. Not on macOS: there only the reap tells."""
        if proc is None:
            return False
        if proc.returncode is not None:
            return True
        if not hasattr(os, "waitid"):
            return False
        try:
            return os.waitid(os.P_PID, proc.pid, os.WEXITED | os.WNOHANG | os.WNOWAIT) is not None
        except ChildProcessError:
            return True  # our own child, already reaped (wait() reaps before it sets returncode): it ended
        except OSError:
            return False

    stopped = []  # signals that came from outside (a stop, a ^C): a requeue never overrides them
    requeue = []  # set by on_requeue: this wrapper is cancelling the run to start it again

    def forward(signum, _frame):
        stopped.append(signum)
        cancel_run(signum)

    def on_requeue(_signum, _frame):
        # SIGUSR1, sent by this wrapper's own throttle thread: cancel the run as a stop would
        if cancelled or stopped or proc is None or leader_ended():
            return
        requeue.append(True)
        cancel_run(signal.SIGTERM)

    def on_limit(_signum, _frame):
        # SIGUSR2, sent by this wrapper's own throttle thread: the run started past a
        # waiting run and its time limit has passed. Cancel it as a stop would, with the reason, and never requeue it
        if cancelled or stopped or proc is None or leader_ended():
            return
        cancel_run(signal.SIGTERM)
        if cancelled:  # cancel_run returns without cancelling when the command has just ended: then there is no stop to name
            rec["stop_reason"] = "time limit"

    def ask_requeue():
        signal.pthread_kill(threading.main_thread().ident, signal.SIGUSR1)

    def ask_stop():
        signal.pthread_kill(threading.main_thread().ident, signal.SIGUSR2)

    def cancel_run(signum):
        if leader_ended():
            return  # the command already ended (its leftovers are being cleared): its code stands
        if proc is not None:
            # a cancelled run never reports success, and a command that traps TERM still ends
            # Only plain os calls here: a handler that starts a thread can deadlock on threading's own lock.
            # SIGALRM does the KILL in 5s.
            if not cancelled:
                cancelled.append(signum)
                # no group_alive here (on macOS it runs ps): the leader isn't reaped yet, so the pgid is still ours.
                # CONT first, in case the throttle has the group stopped; from here on it stops nothing more.
                for sig in (signal.SIGCONT, signal.SIGTERM):
                    try:
                        os.killpg(proc.pid, sig)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                    signal_scope(scope, sig)  # plain file reads and os.kill: safe in a handler
                signal.alarm(5)
        else:
            cancelled.append(signum)  # the finally below removes the holder (and records it, if admitted)
            sys.exit(128 + signum)

    for s in (signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP):
        signal.signal(s, forward)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, escalate)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGUSR1, on_requeue)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGUSR2, on_limit)

    try:
        try:
            # before any slot: a bad --log path fails without holding anything. In the shared logs folder
            # the name is guessable, so a link planted there is refused, never followed.
            nofollow = os.O_NOFOLLOW if os.path.realpath(os.path.dirname(log)) == os.path.realpath(LOGS) else 0
            logf = os.fdopen(os.open(log, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT | nofollow, 0o644), "ab")
            own_mode(logf.fileno(), 0o644)
        except OSError as e:
            die(f"run: log {log}: {e.strerror}", 1)
        try:
            if track:
                write_holder(holder_path, rec)
                held, waited = [], False
                rec.update(state="admitted", since=time.time(), load_at_start=loadavg()[0])
            else:
                held, waited = take_slots(rec, holder_path, heavy)
        except OSError as e:
            if not isinstance(e, PermissionError):
                die(f"{e.filename or DIR}: {e.strerror}", 1)
            die(f"{e.filename or DIR}: permission denied. Is {me} in the testslot group in this shell? "
                f"(`id -nG`; a new group needs a fresh login)", 1)
        if waited:
            print("testslot: slot free, starting", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        env = dict(os.environ, TESTSLOT_LOG=log)
        if not track:
            # A light run marks the slot held too, so a test-parallel inside it runs rather than waiting
            # on its own parent forever: the caller chose light. Wrap full suites with --heavy.
            env.update(TESTSLOT_HELD=f"{kind}:{pid}:{rec['start']}")

        sigs = {signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP}

        def child_setup():
            # a run started from a non-interactive `&` inherits an ignored SIGINT; give the tests the default back
            signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, signal.SIG_DFL)
            signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIG_DFL)
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, sigs)

        # Only this wrapper holds the lock (close_fds keeps it from the command), so the slot ends with
        # the wrapper, however it ends, and nothing the command starts (a daemon, a setsid child) can keep
        # it. A wrapper killed outright leaves its command running without a slot; status says so.
        unit = f"testslot-{pid}-{int(time.time())}"
        argv, why, extra = scope_command(command, unit)
        mark_r = mark_w = None
        if argv is not command:
            mark_r, mark_w = os.pipe()
            os.set_blocking(mark_r, False)
            extra = dict(extra, TESTSLOT_MARKFD=str(mark_w))

        def spawn(a, more, keep=()):
            # signals wait while the child starts, so none lands between "slot taken" and "proc known"
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, sigs)
            try:
                return subprocess.Popen(a, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True,
                                        env=dict(env, **more), preexec_fn=child_setup, pass_fds=keep)
            except OSError as e:
                die(f"run: {command[0]}: {e.strerror}", 127)
            finally:
                signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, sigs)

        proc = spawn(argv, extra, (mark_w,) if mark_w is not None else ())
        if argv is not command:  # systemd-run execs the command itself, so proc.pid is the command and its group
            os.close(mark_w)
            # The launcher answers on the pipe: a byte (the command is about to run), or the end of the file with no
            # byte (it never ran). Only the second may be rerun, once, without a scope. If neither comes in
            # LAUNCH_S the launcher is stopped first, and what its pipe then holds decides: a byte means the command
            # may have started, so it is not run again.
            outcome = read_marker(mark_r, LAUNCH_S)
            if outcome == "timeout":
                try:
                    os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                except OSError:
                    pass
                outcome = read_marker(mark_r, 5)
                os.close(mark_r)
                if outcome == "timeout":
                    die(f"run: systemd-run did not start {command[0]} in {LAUNCH_S:g} s and could not be stopped", 1)
                if outcome == "byte":
                    die(f"run: systemd-run started {command[0]} just as it was stopped; not run again", 1)
                why = f"systemd-run did not start the command in {LAUNCH_S:g} s; it ran without a scope"
            else:
                os.close(mark_r)
                if outcome == "eof":
                    why = "systemd-run failed to start the command; it ran without a scope"
            if outcome == "byte":
                scope, why = find_scope(proc, unit, pid)
            else:
                try:
                    proc.wait(timeout=5)
                except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                    kill_group(proc.pid)
                    proc.wait()
                proc.stdout.close()
                if cancelled:  # a signal came while the launcher ran: no second start
                    sys.exit(128 + cancelled[0])
                proc = spawn(command, {})
        rec.update(state="running", pgid=proc.pid, cgroup=scope, observed_via="cgroup" if scope or ((OBSERVED_FILE or OBSERVED_DIR) and VIA_HOOK == "cgroup") else "group",
                   cgroup_note=why if LINUX else None)
        write_holder(holder_path, rec)
        rec_lock = threading.Lock()
        stop_ev, sample_thread = start_sampler(rec, holder_path, proc.pid, rec_lock, scope)
        throttle = start_throttle(rec, holder_path, proc.pid, rec_lock, scope, lambda: bool(cancelled), stop_ev,
                                  ask_requeue if kind == "light" and not requeued else None,
                                  ask_stop if rec.get("limit_s") else None)
        sampler = (stop_ev, sample_thread, rec_lock, throttle)
        print(f"testslot: {kind} run, {cpus_of(rec)} cpus, pgid {proc.pid}, log {log}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)

        def copy():
            logging = True
            for chunk in iter(lambda: proc.stdout.read1(65536), b""):
                if logging:
                    try:
                        logf.write(chunk)
                        logf.flush()
                    except ValueError:  # closed after the join timed out: a leftover still holds the pipe
                        return
                    except OSError as e:  # disk full: stop logging, keep draining, or the command blocks
                        logging = False
                        try:  # stderr may be on the same full disk
                            print(f"testslot: log write failed ({e.strerror}); output goes to the terminal only",
                                  file=sys.stderr)
                        except OSError:
                            pass
                try:
                    sys.stdout.buffer.write(chunk)
                    sys.stdout.buffer.flush()
                except (BrokenPipeError, OSError):
                    pass

        # The copy thread starts with TERM/INT/HUP, ALRM (the KILL escalation), and USR1/USR2 (requeue/time limit)
        # blocked (a new thread inherits the mask), so the kernel always hands them to the main thread. Otherwise one can land on
        # this thread while the main thread sits in wait(), and the cancel waits for the command to end
        # (seen after a Ctrl-Z).
        t = threading.Thread(target=copy, daemon=True)
        tsigs = sigs | {signal.SIGALRM, signal.SIGUSR1, signal.SIGUSR2}
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, tsigs)
        try:
            t.start()
        finally:
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, tsigs)
        code = proc.wait()
        RAN[0] = 128 + cancelled[0] if cancelled else code if code >= 0 else 128 - code  # main keeps it if cleanup fails
        stop_sampler(sampler)
        if os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_TEST_CRASH") == "after-wait":  # tests only
            raise RuntimeError("after the command ended")
        # Whatever the command left running in its group or cgroup would run on after the slot frees: end it.
        if run_alive(proc.pid):
            end_group(proc.pid)
        ended = True
        t.join(timeout=2)  # a child that left the group but kept the pipe must not hang us
        try:
            logf.close()
        except OSError:  # the full disk again, flushing the last of the buffer: the command's code stands
            pass
        # a command killed by a signal of its own (the OOM killer, say) failed; only a signal to this
        # wrapper, from the caller or from `testslot stop`, makes a cancel
        result = 128 + cancelled[0] if cancelled else code if code >= 0 else 128 - code
        return result
    except SystemExit as e:
        result = e.code if isinstance(e.code, int) else 1
        raise
    finally:
        # however we leave (an exception included), end the sampler first (it must not write the record back
        # after we remove it), then the command's group before the slot frees
        stop_sampler(sampler)
        if proc is not None and (rec.get("paused_since") or rec.get("load_paused_since")):  # the throttle didn't end in time: never leave the group stopped
            try:
                os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGCONT)
            except OSError:
                pass
            signal_scope(scope, signal.SIGCONT)
        if proc is not None and run_alive(proc.pid):
            # once already tried (TERM, 5s, KILL) only a last KILL: a group stuck in the kernel costs no second wait
            kill_group(proc.pid) if ended else end_group(proc.pid)
        # a group that outlives even SIGKILL (stuck in the kernel) keeps its record, so status lists it
        # as running without a slot; prune drops the record once the group is gone
        if rec.get("since"):  # admitted: what happened goes into history, now or when prune finds it
            rec.update(ended=time.time(), exit=result if result is not None else 128 + cancelled[0] if cancelled else None,
                       cancelled=bool(cancelled))
        if proc is None or not run_alive(proc.pid):
            try:
                os.unlink(holder_path)
                if rec.get("since"):
                    append_history(history_line(rec, rec["ended"], rec["exit"]))
            except OSError:
                pass
        elif rec.get("since"):
            try:  # the group outlived SIGKILL: keep the record, with the exit, for prune to write later
                write_holder(holder_path, rec)
            except OSError:
                pass
        if requeue and cancelled and not stopped and rec.get("since") and proc is not None and not run_alive(proc.pid):
            exec_requeue(rec, command, log)


def exec_requeue(rec, command, log):
    """The run was cancelled by its own wrapper for using far more than it declared: start the same
    command again, once, in this process (so the caller keeps waiting on the same pid), at the head of the queue, declaring
    its measured peak. Returns only if the restart could not be made; the caller then ends as a cancel."""
    peak = rec.get("requeue_peak")
    declared = rec.get("requeue_declared")
    if not isinstance(peak, (int, float)) or isinstance(peak, bool) or not math.isfinite(peak) or peak <= 0:
        return
    new = requeue_size(peak)
    line = (f"requeued: this run used {peak:g} cpus against the {declared:g} it declared while the load was "
            f"{(rec.get('requeue_load') or 0):g} on {CORES} cores; stopped, and starting again at the head of the queue declaring {new}")
    print(f"testslot: {line}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    try:  # and in the run's log, so whoever reads the log sees why its output starts over
        with open(log, "ab") as f:
            f.write(f"testslot: {line}\n".encode())
    except OSError:
        pass
    argv = [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(sys.argv[0]), "run", "--cpus", str(new), "--requeued", f"{declared:g}:{peak:g}",
            "--key", str(rec.get("key") or ""), "--log", log, "--"] + list(command)
    if rec.get("limit_s"):
        argv[argv.index("--key"):argv.index("--key")] = ["--max-minutes", f"{rec['limit_s'] / 60:.6g}"]
    try:
        sys.stdout.flush()
        signal.alarm(0)  # the cancel set a 5 s KILL timer; a pending alarm survives exec and would KILL the new run
        os.execv(sys.executable, argv)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"testslot: could not start the run again ({e.strerror}); it ends as a cancel", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


def fmt_limit(s):
    return f"{s / 60:g}m" if s % 60 == 0 else f"{s:g}s"


def fmt_elapsed(t):
    if not t:
        return "-"
    s = int(time.time() - t)
    return f"{s // 3600}h{s % 3600 // 60:02d}m" if s >= 3600 else f"{s // 60}m{s % 60:02d}s"


def job(h, now):
    """One holder as a job record, for status --json."""
    started = h.get("since")
    return {"pid": h.get("pid"), "pgid": h.get("pgid"), "account": h.get("account"), "repo": h.get("repo"),
            "command": h.get("command"), "kind": h.get("kind"), "cpus": cpus_of(h), "state": h.get("state"),
            "observed": valid_observed(h), "observed_via": h.get("observed_via"), "cgroup": h.get("cgroup"), "cgroup_note": h.get("cgroup_note"),
            "charged": charged_cpus(h), "shrunk": valid_observed(h) is not None and shrunk(h, cpus_of(h), valid_observed(h)),
            "shrink_after": record_shrink_after(h),
            "queued": h.get("queued"), "started": started,
            "wait_s": round((started or now) - h["queued"], 1) if h.get("queued") else None,
            "run_s": round(now - started, 1) if started else None,
            "orphan": bool(h.get("orphan")), "backfill": bool(h.get("backfill")), "log": h.get("log"),
            "wait_reason": h.get("wait_reason") if h.get("state") == "waiting" and isinstance(h.get("wait_reason"), str) else "",
            "paused": bool(h.get("paused_since") or h.get("load_paused_since")),
            "pause_reason": [r for r, k in (("load", "load_paused_since"), ("start-rate", "paused_since")) if h.get(k)],
            "pauses": h.get("pauses") or 0, "load_pauses": h.get("load_pauses") or 0, "requeued": h.get("requeued_from"),
            "limit_s": h.get("limit_s") if isinstance(h.get("limit_s"), (int, float)) and not isinstance(h.get("limit_s"), bool) else None,
            "limit_left_s": round(h["limit_s"] - (now - started), 1) if started and isinstance(h.get("limit_s"), (int, float))
                            and not isinstance(h.get("limit_s"), bool) else None}


def snapshot():
    """The queue right now: what runs, what waits (in turn order), the load and the cap."""
    now = time.time()
    hs = read_holders()
    known = {h["pid"] for h in hs} | {h.get("pgid") for h in hs if h.get("pgid")}
    running = [job(h, now) for h in hs if holds_cpus(h)]
    queue = [h for h in hs if h.get("state") == "waiting" and h.get("kind") != "track"]
    live = [h for h in queue if not stopped(h["pid"])]
    waiting = [dict(job(h, now), position=n + 1, stopped=False) for n, h in enumerate(live)]
    waiting += [dict(job(h, now), position=None, stopped=True) for h in queue if stopped(h["pid"])]
    tracked = [job(h, now) for h in hs if h.get("kind") == "track" and h.get("state") != "waiting"]
    outside = []
    for p, how in outside_runs(known):
        acct, cwd, cmd = proc_info(p)
        try:
            pgid = os.getpgid(p)
        except OSError:
            pgid = None
        outside.append({"pid": p, "pgid": pgid, "account": acct, "cwd": cwd, "command": cmd, "how": how,
                        "note": outside_note() if how == "flock" else ""})
    used = sum(j["charged"] for j in running)
    held = reserving(live, now)
    # the host's start rate as the freshest wrapper read it (status itself takes no reading: two are needed, a second apart)
    rates = [(h.get("host_rate_at"), h.get("host_rate")) for h in hs if isinstance(h.get("host_rate_at"), (int, float))
             and isinstance(h.get("host_rate"), (int, float)) and now - h["host_rate_at"] <= 5 * START_RATE_S]
    start_rate = max(rates)[1] if rates else None
    reserved_by = None
    if held:
        reserved_by = next(j for j in waiting if j["pid"] == held["pid"])
    return {"host": os.uname().nodename, "generated_at": round(now, 3), "cores": CORES, "cap": read_cap(), "load_cap": read_load_cap(), "platform": PLATFORM,
            "start_rate_cap": read_start_rate_cap(), "start_rate": start_rate,
            "load": loadavg(), "cpus_in_use": round(used, 2), "cpus_free": round(max(0, read_cap() - used), 2),
            "reserve_s": RESERVE, "reserved_by": reserved_by,
            "running": running, "waiting": waiting, "tracked": tracked, "outside": outside}


def seen_text(j):
    """' (using 7.9)' for a running run whose wrapper has a reading, else nothing."""
    text = f" (using {j['observed']:g})" if j.get("observed") is not None and j.get("state") != "waiting" else ""
    if j.get("shrunk") and j.get("state") != "waiting":
        text += f" [charged {j['charged']:g} of {j['cpus']}: under its size for {secs(j.get('shrink_after') or SHRINK_AFTER_S)} or more]"
    if j.get("cgroup_note") and j.get("state") != "waiting":
        text += f" [counted by process group, not cgroup: {j['cgroup_note']}]"
    return text


def cmd_status(argv):
    if argv not in ([], ["--json"]):
        die("usage: testslot status [--json]")
    snap = snapshot()
    if argv:
        print(json.dumps(snap, indent=1))
        return 0
    load = " ".join(f"{x:.2f}" for x in snap["load"])
    print(f"{os.uname().nodename.split('.')[0]} test slots: load {load} on {CORES} cores; cap {read_cap()} cpus, "
          f"{snap['cpus_in_use']} in use, {snap['cpus_free']} free")
    if snap["start_rate_cap"] is not None:
        now_rate = "not read (no run is reading it)" if snap["start_rate"] is None else f"{snap['start_rate']:g}/s now"
        print(f"start-rate cap {snap['start_rate_cap']:g} process starts/s for the host; {now_rate}")
    if snap["load"][0] >= read_load_cap():
        print(f"closed: the 1-minute load is at or over the load cap; nothing starts until it falls under {read_load_cap():g}")
    if not (snap["running"] or snap["waiting"] or snap["tracked"] or snap["outside"]):
        print("free: nothing running, nothing waiting")
        return 0
    for title, group, clock in (("holding", snap["running"], "started"), ("waiting", snap["waiting"], "queued"),
                                ("tracked (no slot)", snap["tracked"], "started")):
        if group:
            print(f"{title}:")
        for j in group:
            gone = "  (wrapper killed: still running, WITHOUT a slot, still counts against the cap)" if j["orphan"] else ""
            at = time.strftime("%H:%M", time.localtime(j[clock])) if j.get(clock) else "-"
            pos = ("  (stopped)" if j.get("stopped") else f"  #{j['position']}") if "position" in j else ""
            if j.get("backfill"):
                pos += "  (started by backfill)"
            if j.get("limit_s"):
                pos += f"  (limit {fmt_limit(j['limit_s'])}" + (f", {max(j['limit_left_s'], 0):.0f}s left)" if j.get("limit_left_s") is not None else ")")
            if j.get("state") == "waiting" and j.get("wait_reason"):
                pos += f"  (waiting: {j['wait_reason']})"
            if j.get("requeued"):
                r = j["requeued"]
                pos += (f"  (requeued once: declared {r.get('declared', 0):g}, peak {r.get('peak', 0):g}, now {r.get('cpus', 0):g}; "
                        f"a second overrun is left to the load pause)")
            if "load" in (j.get("pause_reason") or []):
                pos += f"  (paused: load {snap['load'][0]:g} on {snap['cores']} cores, using more than it declared)"
            if "start-rate" in (j.get("pause_reason") or []):
                pos += "  (paused: host starts over the start-rate cap)"
            if snap["reserved_by"] and j["pid"] == snap["reserved_by"]["pid"]:
                pos += "  (cpus reserved for it)"
            print(f"  {j['kind']:5}  {j['account']:6}  pgid {str(j['pgid'] or '-'):>7}  {at} {fmt_elapsed(j[clock]):>7}  "
                  f"{j['cpus']} cpus{seen_text(j)}{pos}  {j['repo']}{gone}\n         {j['command']}\n         log {j['log']}")
    if snap["outside"]:
        print("running outside testslot (an flock on the heavy file, or a test-parallel no slot holds):")
        for o in snap["outside"]:
            cmd = o["command"] if len(o["command"]) <= 160 else o["command"][:157] + "..."   # a suite names every module
            print(f"  {o['account']:6}  pgid {str(o['pgid'] or '?'):>7}  {o['cwd']}\n         {cmd}"
                  + (f"\n         its note: {o['note']}" if o["note"] else ""))
    return 0


def parse_span(text):
    """'90s', '30m', '12h', '2d' or plain seconds -> seconds."""
    units = {"s": 1, "m": 60, "h": 3600, "d": 86400}
    try:
        if text[-1:] in units:
            return float(text[:-1]) * units[text[-1]]
        return float(text)
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        die(f"summary: --since wants a span like 30m, 12h or 2d, not {text}")


def read_history(since):
    """History lines that ended at or after `since`, oldest first, from history.jsonl.1 and history.jsonl."""
    out = []
    for path in (HISTORY + ".1", HISTORY):
        try:
            with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                for line in f:
                    try:
                        r = json.loads(line)
                    except ValueError:
                        continue
                    if not isinstance(r, dict):
                        continue
                    ended = r.get("ended") or 0
                    if isinstance(ended, bool) or not isinstance(ended, (int, float)):
                        continue  # a hand-edited or half-written line: skipped, not an error for every reader
                    if ended >= since:
                        out.append(r)
        except OSError:
            pass
    return sorted(out, key=lambda r: r.get("ended") or 0)


def spread(xs):
    """median, p90 and max of a list of seconds, or None for each when empty."""
    xs = sorted(x for x in xs if isinstance(x, (int, float)))
    if not xs:
        return {"median": None, "p90": None, "max": None}
    pick = lambda q: xs[min(len(xs) - 1, int(q * len(xs)))]
    mid = len(xs) // 2
    med = xs[mid] if len(xs) % 2 else (xs[mid - 1] + xs[mid]) / 2
    return {"median": round(med, 1), "p90": round(pick(0.9), 1), "max": round(xs[-1], 1)}


def summarize(span):
    now = time.time()
    since = now - span
    runs = read_history(since)
    timed = [r for r in runs if not r.get("lost")]  # a lost run's end is only when it was found gone
    snap = snapshot()
    # declared cpus over time, 24 buckets across the span: finished runs plus the ones still going
    width = span / 24
    busy = [0.0] * 24
    spans = [(r.get("started"), r.get("ended"), r.get("cpus") or 0) for r in timed]
    spans += [(j["started"], now, j["cpus"]) for j in snap["running"]]
    for s, e, c in spans:
        if not (s and e and c):
            continue
        for b in range(24):
            lo, hi = since + b * width, since + (b + 1) * width
            busy[b] += c * max(0.0, min(e, hi) - max(s, lo)) / width
    ok = [r for r in runs if r.get("exit") == 0]
    cancelled = [r for r in runs if r.get("cancelled") and not r.get("lost")]
    lost = [r for r in runs if r.get("lost")]
    by_repo = {}
    for r in runs:
        by_repo[r.get("repo") or "?"] = by_repo.get(r.get("repo") or "?", 0) + 1
    hist = {"since": round(since, 3), "until": round(now, 3), "runs": len(runs), "ok": len(ok),
            "failed": len(runs) - len(ok) - len(cancelled) - len(lost), "cancelled": len(cancelled), "lost": len(lost), "backfilled": sum(1 for r in runs if r.get("backfill")),
            "wait_s": spread([r.get("wait_s") for r in timed]), "run_s": spread([r.get("run_s") for r in timed]),
            "cpu_s": round(sum((r.get("run_s") or 0) * (r.get("cpus") or 0) for r in timed), 1),
            "by_repo": dict(sorted(by_repo.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1])),
            "cpus_over_time": {"bucket_s": round(width, 1), "cpus": [round(x, 2) for x in busy]}}
    return {"now": snap, "history": hist}


def cmd_summary(argv):
    span, as_json = 12 * 3600, False
    while argv:
        a = argv.pop(0)
        if a == "--json":
            as_json = True
        elif a == "--since" and argv:
            span = parse_span(argv.pop(0))
        else:
            die("usage: testslot summary [--since 12h] [--json]")
    if not (0 < span < float("inf")):
        die("summary: --since must be a span more than zero")
    prune(getpass.getuser())  # so a run whose wrapper was killed shows up without waiting for the next run
    out = summarize(span)
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(out, indent=1))
        return 0
    n, h = out["now"], out["history"]
    load = " ".join(f"{x:.2f}" for x in n["load"])
    print(f"now: load {load} on {CORES} cores; cap {read_cap()} cpus, {n['cpus_in_use']} in use; "
          f"{len(n['running'])} running, {len(n['waiting'])} waiting, {len(n['outside'])} outside testslot")
    for j in n["running"]:
        print(f"  running  {j['account']:6} {j['cpus']:>2} cpus{seen_text(j)}  {fmt_elapsed(j['started']):>7}  {j['repo']}"
              + ("  (started by backfill)" if j.get("backfill") else ""))
    for j in n["waiting"]:
        print(f"  {('#' + str(j['position'])) if j['position'] else 'stop':<3} wait {j['account']:6} {j['cpus']:>2} cpus  {fmt_elapsed(j['queued']):>7}  {j['repo']}"
              + ("  (cpus reserved for it)" if n["reserved_by"] and j["pid"] == n["reserved_by"]["pid"] else ""))
    span = h["until"] - h["since"]
    label = f"{span / 86400:g}d" if span >= 172800 else f"{span / 3600:g}h" if span >= 3600 else f"{span / 60:g}m"
    print(f"last {label}: {h['runs']} runs ({h['ok']} ok, {h['failed']} failed, {h['cancelled']} cancelled, "
          f"{h['lost']} lost), {round(h['cpu_s'] / 3600, 1)} cpu-hours, {h['backfilled']} started by backfill")
    for name in ("wait_s", "run_s"):
        st = h[name]
        label = "wait" if name == "wait_s" else "run "
        print(f"  {label}: median {secs(st['median'])}, p90 {secs(st['p90'])}, longest {secs(st['max'])}")
    bars = " ▁▂▃▄▅▆▇█"
    line = "".join(bars[min(8, round(8 * c / read_cap()))] for c in h["cpus_over_time"]["cpus"])
    print(f"  cpus over time (cap {read_cap()}, oldest left): |{line}| peak {max(h['cpus_over_time']['cpus']):.1f}")
    for repo, count in list(h["by_repo"].items())[:5]:
        print(f"  {count:>4}  {repo}")
    return 0


def secs(x):
    if x is None:
        return "-"
    x = int(x)
    return f"{x // 3600}h{x % 3600 // 60:02d}m" if x >= 3600 else f"{x // 60}m{x % 60:02d}s"


def group_pids(pgid):
    r = subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-g", str(pgid)], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace")
    return r.stdout.split()


def cmd_stop(argv):
    sig = signal.SIGTERM
    if argv and argv[0] == "--kill":
        sig = signal.SIGKILL
        argv = argv[1:]
    if len(argv) != 1 or not argv[0].isdigit():
        die("usage: testslot stop [--kill] <pgid>")
    pgid = int(argv[0])
    # The holder is the one whose wrapper is the parent of the group's leader: a record is group-writable, and one
    # forged to carry another live run's pid must not make stop signal that run or its scope. With the leader gone
    # there is no parent to check, so no wrapper is signalled (the group and an orphan's scope still are).
    cands = [h for h in read_holders() if h.get("pgid") == pgid]
    parent = parent_of(pgid)
    genuine = [h for h in cands if not h.get("orphan") and parent == h["pid"]]
    holder = next(iter(genuine), None) or next((h for h in cands if h.get("orphan")), None)
    scope = holder_scope(holder, bool(genuine)) if holder else None
    if not group_pids(pgid) and not scope_pids(scope):
        print(f"testslot: nothing runs in process group {pgid}")
        return 0
    # A run under testslot is stopped through its wrapper, so the wrapper records a cancel (a signal to
    # the group alone looks like the command dying by itself, which is a failure). The wrapper TERMs the
    # group and KILLs it after 5s; --kill asks the wrapper to KILL it now (SIGALRM), and KILLs it here only
    # if the wrapper hasn't within a second: a KILL sent here first can beat the wrapper's handler, which
    # then sees a leader that ended and records a failure.
    wrapper = next((h["pid"] for h in genuine if alive(h["pid"], h.get("start"))), None)
    # debt: a run stopped in the moment between its start and its holder naming the pgid is signalled
    # as a plain group, so it counts as failed; and a waiting run has no pgid for stop to name.
    # debt: with ps failing (macOS), alive() can't check the start time, so a wrapper pid reused since
    # would get the TERM and CONT. Needs a pid wrap during a ps outage. Upgrade: skip the wrapper then.
    try:
        if wrapper:
            try:
                os.kill(wrapper, signal.SIGTERM)
                if sig == signal.SIGKILL:
                    os.kill(wrapper, signal.SIGALRM)  # its handler records the cancel, then KILLs the group
                os.kill(wrapper, signal.SIGCONT)  # a Ctrl-Z'd wrapper would hold the TERM, and the slot
            except ProcessLookupError:
                wrapper = None  # it ended just now: signal the group itself
        if not wrapper:
            signal_scope(scope, sig)  # children that called setsid are in the cgroup, not the group
            os.killpg(pgid, sig)
            if not wrapper:  # no wrapper to resume it: a group the throttle stopped would hold the TERM pending
                signal_scope(scope, signal.SIGCONT)
                os.killpg(pgid, signal.SIGCONT)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        pass
    except PermissionError:
        acct = proc_info(pgid)[0]
        die(f"process group {pgid} belongs to {acct}; stop it as {acct}, or: sudo testslot stop {pgid}", 1)
    for i in range(20):
        time.sleep(0.5)
        left = group_pids(pgid) + [str(p) for p in scope_pids(scope)]
        if left and i == 1 and sig == signal.SIGKILL and wrapper:
            # the wrapper didn't KILL it within a second (hung, or stopped again): do it here, as before
            signal_scope(scope, sig)
            try:
                os.killpg(pgid, sig)
            except OSError:
                pass
        if not left:
            if wrapper and alive(wrapper):
                continue  # the slot frees when the wrapper ends, not the group
            print(f"testslot: stopped process group {pgid}; nothing left")
            return 0
    if not left:
        print(f"testslot: group {pgid} is gone, but its run (pid {wrapper}) hasn't ended; the slot is still held")
        return 1
    print(f"testslot: still running in group {pgid}: {' '.join(left)}; try: testslot stop --kill {pgid}")
    return 1


def main():
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help", "help"):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    cmds = {"run": cmd_run, "suggest": cmd_suggest, "status": cmd_status, "summary": cmd_summary, "stop": cmd_stop}
    if argv[0] not in cmds:
        die(f"unknown command {argv[0]}; see testslot --help")
    for out in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):  # a path or command line that isn't UTF-8 must print as "?", not crash the listing
        try:
            out.reconfigure(errors="replace")
        except (AttributeError, ValueError):
            pass
    try:
        crash = os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_TEST_CRASH")  # tests only: an internal error, to see the exit code and the stderr line
        if crash == "pipe":
            raise BrokenPipeError()
        if crash and crash != "after-wait":
            raise RuntimeError(crash)
        return cmds[argv[0]](argv[1:])
    except PsFailed:
        die("ps failed three times, so which runs are alive can't be told; nothing was started", 1)
    except BrokenPipeError:  # `testslot status | head`: the reader left, which is not testslot's failure
        try:
            os.dup2(os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY), sys.stdout.fileno())  # so the flush at exit stays quiet
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
        return 141
    except Exception as e:  # SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt are not Exceptions, so exits and ^C pass through
        import traceback
        frames = traceback.extract_tb(e.__traceback__)
        ours = [f for f in frames if os.path.abspath(f.filename) == os.path.abspath(__file__)] or frames
        where = f" (line {ours[-1].lineno})" if ours else ""
        print(f"testslot error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}{where}", file=sys.stderr)
        if os.environ.get("TESTSLOT_DEBUG"):
            traceback.print_exc()
        return INTERNAL_ERROR if RAN[0] is None else RAN[0]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
